const O={};globalThis.P=(en,ar)=>({en,ar});try{globalThis["D"]=O["D"]=({"sites":[{"id":"al-zubarah","n":"Al Zubarah","na":"الزبارة","c":"archaeological","s":"18th-century pearling town · World Heritage","lat":25.978,"lng":51.03,"p":"Late Islamic","dr":"1760s – early 20th century","el":"60 ha · 2.5 km town wall","ic":"wall","era":"H","t0":266,"t1":100,"te":"The town that pearls built","ta":"المدينة التي بناها اللؤلؤ","se":"A walled trading town of 6,000 to 9,000 people, burned in 1811 and buried by sand","sa":"مدينة تجارية مسوّرة سكنها 6 إلى 9 آلاف إنسان، أُحرقت عام 1811 ودفنتها الرمال","desc":"A pearling and trading town founded in the 1760s on the north-west coast, 85 km from Doha. The late 18th-century town wall forms a 2.5 km arc from shore to shore around a 60 ha site that may have held 6,000–9,000 people; a palatial compound of 110 × 100 m has nine courtyards. Forces loyal to the Sultan of Muscat burned the town in 1811, a later town covered about 20% of the area, and the site was abandoned by the early 20th century. Qal'at Murair, a fortified settlement inland, stayed occupied until Al Zubarah Fort was built in 1938. Inscribed on the World Heritage List on 22 June 2013, Qatar's first.","sig":"The best-preserved example of an 18th–19th-century Gulf merchant town, with its street plan, souq, houses and fishing harbours intact under the sand.","ex":"Qatar Museums with the University of Copenhagen (Qatar Islamic Archaeology and Heritage Project), 2009 onwards; Qatari excavations of the souq in the 1980s","disc":["2.5 km town wall from shore to shore","Palatial compound with nine courtyards","Qal'at Murair and the 1938 fort"],"refs":["Qatar Museums (2022). Al Zubarah Archaeological Site: visitor guide.","UNESCO World Heritage Centre. Al Zubarah Archaeological Site (ref. 1402rev), inscribed 2013."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"al-jassasiya","n":"Al Jassasiya Rock Carvings","na":"نقوش الجساسية الصخرية","c":"archaeological","s":"Largest rock-art site in Qatar","lat":26.02,"lng":51.385,"p":"Islamic (dates debated)","dr":"Possibly 11th century AD onwards","el":"c. 900 carvings on 580 numbered rocks","ic":"boat","era":"H","t0":1000,"t1":200,"te":"Boats cut into the rock","ta":"قوارب محفورة في الصخر","se":"Rows of cup marks and 141 boats carved into a coastal ridge","sa":"صفوف من الحفر الفنجانية و141 قاربًا محفورة في ضلع ساحلي","desc":"About 900 carvings across 580 numbered sites on a low ridge 4.8 km south of Fuwairit, discovered in 1961 by a Danish team under Holger Kapel and recorded by Hans Kapel in 1974. Cup-and-ring marks are the most common motif, with animals (ostriches, turtles, fish) and boats: 124 plan drawings and 17 elevation drawings, the only recorded boat carvings in Qatar. Dating is debated: Facey (1987) put the earliest boat drawings at the 11th century AD on the evidence of rudders, Nayeem (1998) suggested the 3rd century BC, and a 2012 dating study found its oldest sample under 300 years old. Nearby habitation mounds hold pottery of the 10th–11th centuries.","sig":"The richest open-air rock-art site on the Gulf coast, and a rare record of the dhows of the pearling age.","ex":"Danish Archaeological Expedition (H. Kapel) 1961; H. Kapel 1974; de Cardi 1973–74","disc":["141 boat carvings in plan and elevation","Cup-and-ring marks in rows and rosettes"],"refs":["Facey, W. (1987). The boat carvings at Jabal Al-Jussasiyah, northeast Qatar. Proceedings of the Seminar for Arabian Studies 17, 199–222.","Hassiba, R. et al. (2012). Determining the age of Qatari Jabal Jassasiyah petroglyphs. QScience Connect 4.","Kapel, H. (1983). Rock carvings at Jabel Jusasiyah, Qatar. Qatar National Museum Journal."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"al-khor-island","n":"Al Khor Island (Purple Island)","na":"جزيرة الخور (الجزيرة الأرجوانية)","c":"archaeological","s":"Kassite purple-dye workshop","lat":25.6928,"lng":51.5489,"p":"Dilmun to Kassite","dr":"c. 2000 – 1100 BC","el":"1.67 km² · outcrop 8.5 m","ic":"shell","era":"P","t0":4025,"t1":3125,"te":"Three million crushed shells","ta":"ثلاثة ملايين صدفة مسحوقة","se":"The oldest known purple-dye works, on an island of mangroves","sa":"أقدم مصنع معروف للصبغة الأرجوانية، على جزيرة من القرم","desc":"A 1.67 km² island in the bay of Al Khor, 50 km north of Doha, joined to the shore by a causeway at low water. Dilmun-period pottery (c. 2000–1750 BC) links it to Bahrain; in the Kassite period (c. 1400–1100 BC) it became a purple-dye works, with about 3,000,000 crushed murex shells (Hexaplex trunculus) and Kassite potsherds, claimed as the earliest known production of shellfish purple. Sasanian and late Islamic material shows later use. First studied systematically in 1976 by the French mission under Jacques Tixier, then by Carter and Killick. Avicennia mangroves line its east and south-east shores.","sig":"The earliest industrial-scale purple-dye site known, tying Qatar to the Kassite kings of Babylon and Dilmun.","ex":"Mission Archéologique Française à Qatar (J. Tixier) 1976; Carter & Killick (2014)","disc":["3,000,000 crushed murex shells","Kassite and Dilmun pottery"],"refs":["Carter, R. & Killick, R. (2014). Al-Khor Island: investigating coastal exploitation in Bronze Age Qatar. Moonrise Press.","Magee, P. (2014). The Archaeology of Prehistoric Arabia. Cambridge University Press."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"wadi-debayan","n":"Wadi Debayan","na":"وادي دبيان","c":"archaeological","s":"Oldest settlement in Qatar","lat":25.945,"lng":51.045,"p":"Neolithic","dr":"c. 7,500 – 4,500 years ago","el":"North-west coast, approximate location","ic":"well","era":"P","t0":7500,"t1":4500,"te":"The first campfires","ta":"أولى نيران المخيم","se":"People lived here 7,500 years ago, until something sudden ended it","sa":"عاش الناس هنا قبل 7,500 عام، حتى أنهى ذلك حدث مفاجئ","desc":"A Neolithic settlement on the north-west coast with occupation from about 7,500 years ago, excavated over three seasons to 2013 by the Qatar National Historic Environment Record project of the University of Birmingham and Qatar Museums (co-director Richard Cuttler). Flint scatters and pottery mark the earliest phase; settlement appears to have ended abruptly shortly after 4,500 years ago, possibly in a high-energy event such as a tsunami.","sig":"Qatar's oldest known settlement, contemporary with the Ubaid-period villages of Kuwait and Saudi Arabia across the Gulf.","ex":"QNHER project, University of Birmingham and Qatar Museums (R. Cuttler, P. Spencer), 2010–2013","disc":["Occupation from about 7,500 years ago","Abrupt end about 4,500 years ago"],"refs":["Gulf Times (13 July 2013). Wadi Debayan offers detailed and rich story of human occupation.","Cuttler, R. et al. Qatar National Historic Environment Record project reports, University of Birmingham."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"murwab","n":"Murwab","na":"مروب","c":"archaeological","s":"Abbasid village with the oldest fort","lat":25.8597,"lng":51.0197,"p":"Early Islamic (Abbasid)","dr":"c. 805 – 885 AD","el":"250 houses · 2 mosques · 1 fort","ic":"jar","era":"H","t0":1220,"t1":1140,"te":"A village of 250 houses","ta":"قرية من 250 بيتًا","se":"An Abbasid village that traded pearls for glazed pots from Basra and Samarra","sa":"قرية عباسية قايضت اللؤلؤ بأوانٍ مزجّجة من البصرة وسامراء","desc":"A 9th-century village 4 km from the west coast and 15 km north of Dukhan: 250 ruined houses, two mosques and a rectangular fort with a central courtyard and twelve rooms, the oldest known fort in Qatar. Of 6,948 potsherds, most date to 805–885 AD, with glazed wares from Susa, Basra or Samarra and no Chinese porcelain; at least 18% of the common pots had been repaired. Discovered by Geoffrey Bibby's Danish team in 1959, excavated by C. Hardy-Guilbert in 1984 and Alexandrine Guérin in 2009. Abandoned in the late 9th century as the Qarmatian revolt began.","sig":"The clearest picture of early Islamic village life in Qatar, and evidence of pearl-for-pottery trade with Iraq.","ex":"Danish expedition (G. Bibby) 1959; French missions 1984 and 2009","disc":["Oldest known fort in Qatar","6,948 potsherds dated 805–885 AD"],"refs":["Guérin, A. & Al-Naimi, F. (2010). Preliminary pottery study: Murwab horizon in progress, ninth century AD, Qatar. Proceedings of the Seminar for Arabian Studies 40.","Hardy-Guilbert, C. (1991). Dilmun and Qatar in the early Islamic period. In: The Heritage of Qatar."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"barzan-towers","n":"Barzan Towers","na":"أبراج برزان","c":"archaeological","s":"Watchtowers of Umm Salal Mohammed","lat":25.418,"lng":51.4131,"p":"Modern","dr":"Late 19th century; renovated 1910","el":"16 m high","ic":"wall","era":"H","t0":140,"t1":23,"te":"Towers that watched the moon","ta":"أبراج راقبت القمر","se":"Built to guard a rainwater valley, watch the sea and sight the Ramadan moon","sa":"بُنيت لحراسة وادٍ لمياه الأمطار، ومراقبة البحر، ورؤية هلال رمضان","desc":"Two watchtowers of coral rock and limestone with mud mortar, 16 m high with walls about 1 m thick at the base, on the low hill of Jebel Lusail at Umm Salal Mohammed, 15 km north of Doha and 10 km from the coast. Built in the late 19th century and renovated in 1910 by Sheikh Mohammed bin Jassim Al Thani, they guarded the rawda, a depression that collected rainwater, watched for Ottoman troops and approaching ships, and were used to sight the new moon of Ramadan. Restored in 2003 and now a museum.","sig":"A rare surviving example of Qatar's pre-oil defensive architecture, tied to water rather than to a coast.","ex":"Qatar Museums (restoration 2003)","disc":["Walls 1 m thick of coral rock and limestone","Rawda rainwater depression below the towers"],"refs":["Hawker, R. (2008). Traditional Architecture of the Arabian Gulf. WIT Press.","Qatar Museums. Barzan Towers (qm.org.qa)."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"zekreet-mesas","n":"Zekreet Peninsula and Mushroom Rocks","na":"شبه جزيرة زكريت وصخور الفطر","c":"geological","s":"Eroded Eocene mesas","lat":25.52,"lng":50.86,"p":"Eocene (Dammam Formation)","dr":"Rocks c. 48 – 40 million years old","el":"Abarug member: 2 m limestone over 10 m marl","ic":"cliff","era":"D","t0":48000000.0,"t1":0,"te":"Tables and mushrooms of stone","ta":"طاولات وفطر من حجر","se":"Wind and salt carved mesas and mushroom rocks from a layered Eocene sea floor","sa":"نحتت الرياح والملح موائد وصخور فطر من قاع بحر إيوسيني مطبّق","desc":"A peninsula north of Dukhan on the west coast, defined by the half-moon Bay of Zekreet and lying inside the Al Reem Biosphere Reserve. Foothills, mesas and gypsum plateaux are carved from the Abarug member of the Eocene Dammam Formation: an upper 2 m of dull dolomitic limestone over 10 m of brightly coloured dolomitic marl, so that the resistant cap protects pillars and mushroom rocks as the soft marl erodes. Ostriches, gazelles and reintroduced oryx roam the Ras Abrouq headland, and flamingos feed in the bay. Richard Serra's East-West/West-East steel plates stand in a valley here. Tourism has caused litter and vegetation damage.","sig":"Qatar's most striking natural landscape and a clear lesson in differential erosion of the Eocene sea floor.","ex":"Qatar Natural History Group; UNESCO Doha (Al Reem studies)","disc":["Hard 2 m limestone cap over soft 10 m marl","Mesas, pillars and mushroom rocks"],"refs":["Leblanc, J. (2008). A fossil hunting guide to the Tertiary formations of Qatar.","Richtzenhain, M. et al. (2008). Towards environmentally friendly tourism in Arabian biosphere reserves: case study Al Reem, Qatar. UNESCO Doha."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"dukhan-field","n":"Dukhan Anticline and Oil Field","na":"طيّة دخان وحقل النفط","c":"geological","s":"Qatar’s first oil, 1940","lat":25.42,"lng":50.78,"p":"Jurassic reservoirs under an Eocene fold","dr":"Discovered January 1940","el":"Anticline 80 km long · well 1,733 m deep","ic":"oil","era":"N","t0":88,"t1":0,"te":"The hill of smoke","ta":"تل الدخان","se":"An 80 km fold at the surface led drillers to Jurassic oil 1,733 m down","sa":"طيّة بطول 80 كم على السطح قادت الحفّارين إلى نفط جوراسي على عمق 1,733 م","desc":"A north–south anticline about 80 km long and up to 8 km wide on the west coast, whose Middle Eocene limestone limbs dip 2–10° with about 90 m of surface closure; the hazy hills were called Jabal Dukhan, the hill of smoke. Dukhan No. 1 was spudded in October 1938 and completed in January 1940 at 1,733 m, flowing about 4,480 barrels a day from Upper Jurassic limestone (the Zekrit Formation, equivalent to the Arab Formation) rather than the expected Cretaceous. The first cargo, 80,000 tons, left Umm Said on 31 December 1949. Non-associated gas was found in the Permian Khuff Formation at about 3,000 m in 1959–60, with production from 1978.","sig":"The structure that made modern Qatar, and a textbook case of surface geology pointing to deep oil.","ex":"Petroleum Development (Qatar) Ltd, 1938–1940; QatarEnergy","disc":["Discovery well completed January 1940 at 1,733 m","Khuff gas at 3,000 m, 1959–60"],"refs":["Sorkhabi, R. (2010). The Qatar oil discoveries. GEO ExPro."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"qatar-arch","n":"The Qatar Arch","na":"قوس قطر","c":"geological","s":"The fold that lifted the peninsula","lat":24.93,"lng":51.04,"p":"Eocene to Pleistocene","dr":"Rocks 56 million years old to recent","el":"Highest point 103 m (south-west)","ic":"fault","era":"D","t0":56000000.0,"t1":0,"te":"Why Qatar is above the sea","ta":"لماذا قطر فوق البحر","se":"A long, gentle arch of the Arabian plate holds the whole peninsula out of the water","sa":"قوس طويل لطيف في الصفيحة العربية يرفع شبه الجزيرة كلها فوق الماء","desc":"Qatar sits on the eastern stable shelf of the Arabian Plate, lifted by the Qatar–South Fars Arch, a regional NNE–SSW anticline bounded to the north by the Zagros belt. The surface is mostly Lower Eocene to Pleistocene limestone and dolostone with marl, shale, clay and gypsum, plus Upper Miocene–Pliocene gravels and conglomerates; the highest point is 103 m in the south-west, and aeolian dunes cover about 12% of the country. The same arch carries the Dukhan field and the offshore North Field.","sig":"The single structure that explains Qatar's existence, its flatness, its caves and its hydrocarbons.","ex":"Al-Awah & Matter (2023); Qatar geological surveys","disc":["Peninsula lifted on a NNE–SSW arch","Dunes on 12% of the land"],"refs":["Al-Awah, H. & Matter, W. S. (2023). Physicochemical characterization and origin of aeolian sand dunes in southeastern Qatar. Advances in Geosciences 63, 1–13."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"dahl-al-misfir","n":"Dahl Al Misfir Cave","na":"دحل المسفر","c":"geological","s":"Gypsum cave that glows","lat":25.25,"lng":51.21,"p":"Quaternary karst in Eocene rock","dr":"Limestone 40 – 50 million years old; cave younger","el":"40 m deep · entrance 12 × 4.5 m","ic":"sinkhole","era":"D","t0":2580000.0,"t1":0,"te":"The cave that glows","ta":"الكهف الذي يتوهج","se":"Fibrous gypsum crystals light a 40 m sinkhole in the central desert","sa":"بلورات جبس ليفية تضيء دحلًا بعمق 40 م في الصحراء الوسطى","desc":"A sinkhole cave about 40 km south-west of Doha near Rawdat Rashid, 40 m deep (at least 100 m by one account) with an entrance of about 12 × 4.5 m. Its fibrous gypsum crystals, derived from the Lower Dammam and Rus Formations, give the interior a faint phosphorescent glow; the surrounding Eocene limestone is 40–50 million years old and the cave formed later by dissolution, part of the Middle Pleistocene karst of Qatar. Fenced, free to enter and rehabilitated in 2022 with paths and a cave map.","sig":"The deepest accessible dahl in Qatar and the best place to see the gypsum that underlies the peninsula.","ex":"Ministry of Environment and Climate Change (rehabilitation 2022)","disc":["Fibrous gypsum with a phosphorescent glow","Middle Pleistocene karst formation"],"refs":["Sadiq, A. M. & Nasir, S. J. (2002). Middle Pleistocene karst evolution in the State of Qatar, Arabian Gulf. Journal of Cave and Karst Studies 64(2), 132–139."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"mesaieed-dunes","n":"Mesaieed Dunes and Khor Al Adaid Sands","na":"كثبان مسيعيد ورمال خور العديد","c":"geological","s":"Mobile dunes marching to the sea","lat":24.9,"lng":51.4,"p":"Holocene","dr":"Active today; sand supply nearly exhausted","el":"Barchans and parabolic dunes","ic":"dune","era":"D","t0":11700,"t1":0,"te":"Dunes on their last journey","ta":"كثبان في رحلتها الأخيرة","se":"Wind drives the sand south-east into the Inland Sea, and no new sand arrives","sa":"تدفع الريح الرمال جنوبًا شرقًا إلى البحر الداخلي، ولا تصل رمال جديدة","desc":"Mobile barchan and parabolic dunes south of Mesaieed, driven by the north-north-westerly shamal toward the Inland Sea and eastern Saudi Arabia. Vegetation cover is 0–1%, far below the 8% that normally checks wind erosion, and the sand supply is described as limited to exhausted: at current rates the dunes could be gone in roughly 500–1,500 years. Dunes cover about 12% of Qatar and are best developed here in the south.","sig":"A dune field with an expiry date, and the sand that gives Khor Al Adaid its spectacle.","ex":"UNESCO Doha (B. Boer); Al-Awah & Matter (2023)","disc":["Vegetation cover 0–1%","Sand supply limited to exhausted"],"refs":["James, B. (2011). Dunes: shifting sands of time. Gulf Times, 13 March 2011.","Al-Awah, H. & Matter, W. S. (2023). Physicochemical characterization and origin of aeolian sand dunes in southeastern Qatar. Advances in Geosciences 63."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"khor-al-adaid","n":"Khor Al Adaid (Inland Sea)","na":"خور العديد","c":"ecology","s":"Tidal inland sea · UNESCO tentative list","lat":24.714,"lng":51.415,"p":"Living today","dr":"On the Tentative List since 2008","el":"Lagoon 15 km × 12 km · channel 10 km","ic":"delta","era":"N","t0":30,"t1":0,"te":"Where the desert meets the sea","ta":"حيث تلتقي الصحراء بالبحر","se":"A tidal lagoon with no equal, ringed by dunes that spill into the water","sa":"بحيرة مدّية لا نظير لها، تحيط بها كثبان تنهال في الماء","desc":"A tidal embayment in the south-east about 15 km north–south and up to 12 km east–west, linked to the Gulf by a channel of about 10 km, submitted to UNESCO's Tentative List on 18 March 2008 under natural criteria (vii) and (viii). Barchan and parabolic dunes reach and spill into the sea; inland and coastal sabkhas prograde seaward under the shamal, salt hummocks record Holocene sea-level change, and dolomite precipitates beneath the surface. Dugongs, turtles, corals and seagrass live in the lagoon, ospreys nest on islets, migrant waterfowl winter here, and Arabian gazelles use the shore.","sig":"The only lagoon system of its kind, where active dunes, sabkhas and a tidal sea meet in one protected landscape.","ex":"Ministry of Environment and Climate Change; UNESCO Tentative List ref. 5317","disc":["Dunes spilling directly into tidal water","Dolomite forming today beneath the sabkha"],"refs":["UNESCO World Heritage Centre. Khor Al-Adaid natural reserve, Tentative List ref. 5317 (2008)."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"al-reem-reserve","n":"Al Reem Biosphere Reserve","na":"محمية الريم","c":"ecology","s":"UNESCO biosphere reserve","lat":25.75,"lng":51.0,"p":"Living today","dr":"Established 2005; UNESCO 2007","el":"120,000 ha, about 10% of Qatar","ic":"gazelle","era":"N","t0":21,"t1":0,"te":"A tenth of the country, kept wild","ta":"عُشر البلاد، حُفظ بريًا","se":"Oryx and gazelle on the limestone plains of the north-west","sa":"المها والغزال على سهول الشمال الغربي الجيرية","desc":"A semi-arid reserve of 120,000 ha in the north-west, about 65 km from Doha, established in 2005 and recognised by UNESCO's Man and the Biosphere programme in 2007. It is an inhabited landscape of limestone plains, mesas and coast, including the Zekreet peninsula and Ras Abrouq, with Arabian oryx and gazelles among its wildlife and vegetation valued by local communities.","sig":"One of 24 UNESCO biosphere reserves in the Middle East and the frame for Qatar's wildlife reintroductions.","ex":"Ministry of Environment and Climate Change; UNESCO MAB","disc":["120,000 ha, 10% of the land area","Arabian oryx and gazelle"],"refs":["UNESCO MAB Biosphere Reserves Directory. Al-Reem (Qatar).","Sillitoe, P., Alshawi, A. A. & Al-Amir Hassan, A. K. (2010). Challenges to conservation: land use change and local participation in the Al Reem Biosphere Reserve, west Qatar. Journal of Ethnobiology and Ethnomedicine 6, 28."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"al-shaheen-whale-sharks","n":"Al Shaheen Whale Shark Aggregation","na":"تجمّع قرش الحوت في الشاهين","c":"ecology","s":"One of the largest whale shark gatherings","lat":26.6,"lng":51.9,"p":"Living today","dr":"Surveyed 2011 onwards; May – August peak","el":"90 km offshore · 60 m water","ic":"dolphin","era":"N","t0":20,"t1":0,"te":"A hundred giants in one square kilometre","ta":"مئة عملاق في كيلومتر مربع واحد","se":"Whale sharks gather around oil platforms to feed on tuna eggs","sa":"تتجمع أقراش الحوت حول منصات النفط لتأكل بيض التونة","desc":"Around the Al Shaheen oil field about 90 km offshore in 60 m of water, whale sharks gather from April to September with a peak from May to August. In 2011 densities reached 100 sharks per km², about 100 were counted on 9 July, 178 in June, and groups of 40 were seen from the platforms; the sharks averaged 6.9 m long and most were male. They feed at the surface on fish eggs identified by DNA as mackerel tuna, which spawn around the platforms as if around fish-aggregating devices.","sig":"One of the largest whale shark aggregations known anywhere, created by an unexpected link between oil platforms and spawning tuna.","ex":"Qatar Ministry of Environment, Maersk Oil Research and Technology Centre (Robinson et al. 2013)","disc":["Up to 100 sharks per km²","Feeding on mackerel tuna eggs"],"refs":["Robinson, D. P., Jaidah, M. Y., Jabado, R. W. et al. (2013). Whale sharks, Rhincodon typus, aggregate around offshore platforms in Qatari waters of the Arabian Gulf to feed on fish spawn. PLoS ONE 8(3), e58255."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"fuwairit-turtles","n":"Fuwairit Turtle Beach","na":"شاطئ فويرط للسلاحف","c":"ecology","s":"Hawksbill nesting beach","lat":26.03,"lng":51.37,"p":"Living today","dr":"Protected nesting season March – July","el":"Designated nesting site since 2020","ic":"wave","era":"N","t0":23,"t1":0,"te":"Where the hawksbills come home","ta":"حيث تعود سلاحف منقار الصقر","se":"219 turtles nested in 2025 and 8,213 hatchlings reached the sea","sa":"عشّشت 219 سلحفاة عام 2025 ووصل 8,213 فرخًا إلى البحر","desc":"The main hawksbill turtle nesting beach of Qatar, designated in 2020 and closed to the public each nesting season from the end of March to July. In 2025, 219 turtles nested on eight beaches (Fuwairit, Ras Rakan, Ras Laffan, Sharawa, Umm Tais, Al Ghariyah, Al Maroona and Al Khor), nests were relocated to Fuwairit and Ras Laffan, and 8,213 hatchlings were released; more than 60,000 hatchlings have been released in five years. The Hawksbill Turtle Conservation Project has run since 2003 with QatarEnergy and Qatar University's Environmental Science Centre.","sig":"The northern Gulf's most important hawksbill rookery, in one of the hottest nesting environments on Earth.","ex":"Ministry of Environment and Climate Change, QatarEnergy, Qatar University ESC","disc":["219 nesting turtles in 2025","8,213 hatchlings released in 2025"],"refs":["Gulf Times (2025). 2025 nesting season for hawksbill turtles ends.","Chatting, M. et al. Nesting ecology of hawksbill turtles, Eretmochelys imbricata, in an extreme environmental setting. Qatar University / Bangor University."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"al-thakhira-mangroves","n":"Al Thakhira Mangrove Reserve","na":"محمية قرم الذخيرة","c":"ecology","s":"Largest mangrove forest in Qatar","lat":25.73,"lng":51.55,"p":"Living today","dr":"Qatar’s mangroves total about 14 km²","el":"Over 7 km² in the reserve","ic":"mangrove","era":"N","t0":30,"t1":0,"te":"Half of Qatar’s mangroves","ta":"نصف قرم قطر","se":"A grey-green forest in the tide, storing carbon and sheltering fish","sa":"غابة رمادية خضراء في المدّ، تخزّن الكربون وتؤوي الأسماك","desc":"The largest mangrove habitat in Qatar, north of Al Khor on the east coast: more than 7 km² of the country's roughly 14 km² of mangroves lie inside the Al Thakhira Reserve, with other stands at Al Khor, Al Wakra, Mesaieed, Al Alia Island, Fuwairit, Al Ruwais, Al Arish and Al Jamil. The Ministry of Environment runs afforestation and restoration programmes based on studies of tide, soil and salinity, citing a global estimate of about 3,754 tonnes of CO₂ stored per hectare including soils.","sig":"A nursery for fish and a carbon store on a coast that has lost most of its natural shoreline.","ex":"Ministry of Environment and Climate Change","disc":["Over 7 km² of mangrove in one reserve","Restoration planting monitored for survival"],"refs":["Qatar Tribune (26 July 2026). Qatar's mangrove forests span 14 sq km as ministry strengthens conservation drive."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"qatar-dugongs","n":"Dugongs of Western Qatar","na":"أطوم غرب قطر","c":"ecology","s":"Record herds on the seagrass","lat":25.6,"lng":50.75,"p":"Living today","dr":"Surveys 2015 – 2016","el":"Waters under 10 m deep","ic":"dolphin","era":"N","t0":12,"t1":0,"te":"Five hundred in a square kilometre","ta":"خمسمئة في كيلومتر مربع","se":"The largest dugong herds outside Australia graze off the west coast","sa":"أكبر قطعان الأطوم خارج أستراليا ترعى قبالة الساحل الغربي","desc":"In the shallow seagrass waters off west and north-west Qatar, helicopter surveys in late 2015 and early 2016 counted 508 dugongs within an area under 1 km², with cow–calf pairs 9.9% of the group, and two foraging herds of 226 and 166 in December 2015. The waters between Qatar and Bahrain form an Important Marine Mammal Area of 10,676 km² holding the world's second-largest dugong population; winter surveys in 2004 had recorded groups of 577 and 97.","sig":"The clearest evidence that the Gulf's dugong population persists at the size first recorded in the 1980s.","ex":"Marshall et al. (2018); Preen (2004)","disc":["508 dugongs in under 1 km²","Herds of 226 and 166 in December 2015"],"refs":["Marshall, C. D. et al. (2018). Large dugong aggregations persist in coastal Qatar. Marine Mammal Science 34(4), 1154–1163.","Preen, A. (2004). Distribution, abundance and conservation status of dugongs and dolphins in the southern and western Arabian Gulf. Biological Conservation 118, 205–218."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"al-wajbah-fort","n":"Al Wajbah Fort","na":"قلعة الوجبة","c":"archaeological","s":"Fort of the 1893 battle","lat":25.3017,"lng":51.3941,"p":"Modern","dr":"Late 18th – 19th century","el":"15 km west of Doha","ic":"sword","era":"H","t0":230,"t1":133,"te":"The day the Ottomans turned back","ta":"يوم تراجع العثمانيون","se":"A small square fort where Sheikh Jassim’s men defeated an Ottoman force in 1893","sa":"قلعة مربعة صغيرة هزم فيها رجال الشيخ جاسم قوة عثمانية عام 1893","desc":"A fort in Al Wajbah, about 15 km west of Doha, thought to date from the late 18th or 19th century. In 1893, after Sheikh Jassim bin Mohammed Al Thani stopped paying taxes to the Ottomans, a contingent sent against him was defeated here and retreated to Doha, three hours away; the battle is remembered as the founding moment of modern Qatar. Restored to its original state in 1990 by the Museums and Archaeology Department, which removed later alterations.","sig":"The site of the battle that secured Al Thani rule and the independence of the peninsula from Ottoman control.","ex":"National Council for Culture, Arts and Heritage (restoration 1990)","disc":["Battle of Al Wajbah, 1893","Restored to original form in 1990"],"refs":["Al-Kholaifi, M. J. (2006). Traditional Architecture in Qatar. pp. 39–41.","Qatar News Agency (18 November 2022). Al Wajbah Fort: a landmark in Qatar's history and present."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"ruwayda","n":"Ruwayda (Al Ruwaida)","na":"الرويضة","c":"archaeological","s":"Fortified coastal town of the 16th–18th centuries · Qatar Museums heritage site","lat":26.08,"lng":51.15,"p":"Late Islamic","dr":"c. 1500s – 1760s","el":"Largest fort in Qatar · 2.25 ha with its outer wall","ic":"wall","era":"H","t0":526,"t1":250,"te":"The fort that grew eight times","ta":"القلعة التي كبرت ثماني مرات","se":"A 2.5 km coastal town with the largest fort in Qatar, two mosques, a boatyard and warehouses, deserted when its people moved to Al Zubarah","sa":"مدينة ساحلية بطول 2.5 كم تضم أكبر قلعة في قطر ومسجدين وورشة سفن ومخازن، هُجرت حين انتقل أهلها إلى الزبارة","desc":"A ruined town on the north-west coast between Al Zubarah and Al Ruwais, stretching about 2.5 km along the shore, with a central square fort of two complexes covering about 2.25 ha including its outer wall. The fort was built in three phases, the last expansion making it eight times its original size; one solid corner tower about 4 m tall may have held a cannon. Discovered by Beatrice de Cardi's British mission in 1973, it was excavated in 2008–2014 by the University of Wales (Lampeter/Trinity Saint David) under Andrew Petersen with the Qatar Museums Authority, uncovering a seafront mosque rebuilt five times, an eight-room warehouse with a date press, a boat workshop, a well and a tomb. Finds include Iranian glazed pottery of the late 16th–early 17th century, 18th-century Chinese ceramics and a 5 cm bone plaque carved with an oryx, about 300 years old. Local informants told Lorimer the town was deserted around the 1760s when residents moved to Al Zubarah.","sig":"The largest fort in Qatar and the best-studied of the pre-Zubarah coastal towns, showing a trading community whose pottery came from Iran and China.","ex":"Beatrice de Cardi (1973); University of Wales Trinity Saint David (A. Petersen) with Qatar Museums Authority, 2008–2014","disc":["Fort of 2.25 ha, enlarged eight times over","Bone plaque carved with an oryx, c. 300 years old"],"refs":["Petersen, A. et al. (2016). Ruwayda: an historic urban settlement in north Qatar. Post-Medieval Archaeology 50(2), 321–349.","Gillespie, F. (5 May 2013). An ancient settlement in north Qatar. Gulf Times.","Qatar Museums. Al Ruwaida Archaeological Site (heritage-sites page)."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"freiha","n":"Freiha (Al Furayhah)","na":"فريحة","c":"archaeological","s":"17th–18th-century fortified village north of Al Zubarah","lat":25.9953,"lng":51.0264,"p":"Late Islamic","dr":"17th century – 1937","el":"Village of c. 50 ha · 700 m along the shore","ic":"boat","era":"H","t0":426,"t1":89,"te":"The village before Zubarah","ta":"القرية التي سبقت الزبارة","se":"A fishing village with a fort, a mosque and 303 rock carvings, at its peak before its famous neighbour","sa":"قرية صيد فيها قلعة ومسجد و303 نقوش صخرية، بلغت ذروتها قبل جارتها الشهيرة","desc":"A deserted village on a shallow bay 3 km north of Al Zubarah, covering about 50 ha and extending some 700 m along the coast and 200 m inland. Excavations and documents put its peak in the 17th–18th centuries, probably before Zubarah; Lorimer counted about 150 inhabitants, mostly fishermen, in 1904, and it was occupied by Qatari forces in July 1937. Qal'at Freiha, a fortified building with corner towers, store rooms and date presses, was excavated and conserved in 2005; from 2009 the Qatar Islamic Archaeology and Heritage Project of Qatar Museums and the University of Copenhagen mapped the site and excavated the central mosque (mihrab, minbar, washing well and courtyard), houses and middens. Stone fishing weights, fish bones and tidal fish traps show a marine economy. Bibby and Glob recorded several hundred cup-marks 5–23 cm wide here in 1956, and Hans Kapel catalogued 303 carvings in 1983.","sig":"A complete small coastal settlement of the pearling age, older than Al Zubarah, excavated as part of the World Heritage site's hinterland.","ex":"Qatari archaeologists (2005); Qatar Museums and University of Copenhagen, QIAH project (A. Walmsley, T. Richter, G. Rees), 2009 onwards","disc":["Fort with date presses, conserved 2005","303 rock carvings recorded in 1983"],"refs":["Rees, G., al-Naimi, F., Richter, T., Bystron, A. & Walmsley, A. (2012). Archaeological excavations at the settlement of al-Furayhah (Freiha), north-west Qatar. Proceedings of the Seminar for Arabian Studies 42, 321–328.","Rees, G., Walmsley, A. & Richter, T. (2011). Investigations in the Zubarah hinterland at Murayr and Furayhah. PSAS 41, 309–316.","Wikipedia contributors (2026). Freiha, citing Lorimer (1904) and Qatar Museums (2016)."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"ain-mohammed","n":"Ain Mohammed","na":"عين محمد","c":"archaeological","s":"Early Islamic workshop settlement · Landscapes of Faith excavation","lat":26.0038,"lng":51.0541,"p":"Early Islamic (Abbasid)","dr":"c. 780 – 830 AD","el":"24 structures · one of only 5 large clustered sites of the period","ic":"jar","era":"H","t0":1246,"t1":1196,"te":"Abbasid workshops in the desert","ta":"ورش عباسية في الصحراء","se":"Paired rooms with plastered basins and platforms, probably for washing and dyeing wool 1,200 years ago","sa":"غرف متجاورة بأحواض ومنصات مجصّصة، ربما لغسل الصوف وصبغه قبل 1,200 عام","desc":"An abandoned village 1.5 km south-west of Freiha with 24 structures including two mosques, a fort and the masonry well that Lorimer recorded in 1908, and an Early Islamic site excavated since winter 2022 by the Qatar Museums Department of Archaeology's Landscapes of Faith project directed by Robert Carter. Occupation is dated by pottery and glass to about 780–830 AD. The main building was planned as three production units of paired rooms with gypsum-plastered basins and platforms, drains through the walls and no kitchens, resembling small industrial units; the team proposes textile work such as washing and dyeing wool. Most pottery was imported from southern Iraq, with wares from Iran, Bahrain, Oman and a few pieces from China; food remains include dates, wheat, barley, dugong and large quantities of pearl oysters. Qatar has more than 30 known sites of the 7th–9th centuries, but only five large clustered ones like Ain Mohammed and nearby Mesaika (c. 750–780 AD).","sig":"Evidence that early Abbasid Qatar had purpose-built manufacturing settlements supplied from Iraq, not just villages.","ex":"Qatar Museums Department of Archaeology, Landscapes of Faith project (R. Carter), 2022 onwards; open days for the public","disc":["Plastered basins and platforms in paired rooms","Pottery mainly from Abbasid Iraq, a few sherds from China"],"refs":["Qatar Museums (2024). Landscapes of Faith: Project Summary (R. Carter).","The Peninsula (24 Feb 2026). Qatar Museums Archaeology Department announces open days at Ain Mohammed and Mesaika excavation sites.","Gulf Times (30 Nov 2023). QM invites public to visit Ain Mohammed archaeological site."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"al-daasa","n":"Al Da'asa","na":"الدعاسة","c":"archaeological","s":"Largest Ubaid-period site in Qatar · west coast","lat":25.3694,"lng":50.8089,"p":"Neolithic (Ubaid period)","dr":"Neolithic, Ubaid period","el":"Nearly 60 hearths","ic":"flame","era":"P","t0":7500,"t1":6000,"te":"Sixty fires by the sea","ta":"ستون موقدًا على البحر","se":"A seasonal camp of fishers and hunters where painted Ubaid pottery from Mesopotamia was found in 1961","sa":"مخيم موسمي لصيادين وجامعي ثمار عُثر فيه عام 1961 على فخار عبيدي مزخرف من بلاد الرافدين","desc":"A Neolithic settlement on the west coast of Qatar, described as the most extensive Ubaid site in the country. It was discovered in 1961 by the Danish team of Holger Kapel, the largest of several flint sites they excavated in 1960–64, and a British team then identified the potsherds as Ubaid; Beatrice de Cardi's team worked here in 1978. Finds include many painted Ubaid sherds, quern fragments, flint scrapers, blades and arrowheads, a carnelian bead and three fragments of red non-Ubaid pottery, along with nearly sixty hearths and postholes for tents or huts. It is interpreted as a seasonal encampment of a hunting, fishing and gathering group that returned repeatedly; the stone tools match Arabian rather than Mesopotamian traditions. A village for Dukhan oil workers was founded on the spot in 1956.","sig":"Qatar's principal link to the Ubaid world of southern Mesopotamia, showing seaborne contact across the Arabian Gulf in the Neolithic.","ex":"Danish Archaeological Expedition (H. Kapel) 1961; B. de Cardi 1978","disc":["Painted Ubaid pottery identified after 1961","Nearly 60 hearths and tent postholes"],"refs":["Wikipedia contributors (2026). Al Da'asa, citing Rice, M. (1994) The Archaeology of the Arabian Gulf and Abdul Nayeem, M. (1998) Qatar Prehistory and Protohistory.","Casey, P. & Vine, P. (1991). The Heritage of Qatar. Immel."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"umm-al-maa","n":"Umm Al Maa burial ground","na":"أم الماء","c":"archaeological","s":"Cairn field of hundreds of tombs · Bronze Age to Sasanian","lat":25.8174,"lng":50.9905,"p":"Bronze Age to Sasanian (dates debated)","dr":"c. 3000 BC – 7th century AD","el":"Mounds 3–6 m across · hundreds, possibly thousands","ic":"mound","era":"P","t0":5025,"t1":1400,"te":"Mother of water, field of cairns","ta":"أم الماء، حقل الركامات","se":"Low stone mounds over graves spread across a well-watered depression, dug by Danish, Japanese and German teams","sa":"ركامات حجرية منخفضة فوق قبور تنتشر في منخفض غني بالماء، نقّب فيها دنماركيون ويابانيون وألمان","desc":"An archaeological area on the west coast about 80 km north-west of Doha, named for its freshwater wells; Lorimer in 1908 noted good masonry wells and a ruined fort built by a Shaikh of Bahrain. Most activity is concentrated in the Mleiha depression of about 25 km², where burial mounds 3–6 m in diameter and 10 cm to 1 m high are capped with limestone, many damaged by robbers, weathering and off-road driving. Bibby and Glob found 50 cairns in 1957, one with a stone cist, and dated pit-fired pottery and alabaster bowl sherds to about 3000 BC by comparison with Bahrain; de Cardi's team found Indian red polished ware and glazed pottery of the Sasanian period (3rd–7th century AD); a Japanese mission (Konishi, Gotoh, Akashi, 1988–91) excavated cists with beads, bronze rings and pottery and proposed a date around the 1st century BC–AD; a German survey led by Juergen Schreiber in 2008 estimated hundreds, possibly thousands, of tombs and saw them as the work of nomads.","sig":"Qatar's largest ancient cemetery, whose contested dates span three millennia of nomadic life on the peninsula.","ex":"Danish expedition (G. Bibby, P. Glob) 1957; B. de Cardi; Japanese mission 1988–91; German survey (J. Schreiber) 2008","disc":["50 cairns counted in 1957, hundreds or thousands by 2008","Sasanian Indian red polished ware"],"refs":["Schreiber, J., Daroczi, T.-T., Muhle, B. & Ewersen, J. (2008/2009). Excavations at the burial ground of Umm al-Ma', Qatar: preliminary report on the first season.","Abdul Nayeem, M. (1998). Qatar Prehistory and Protohistory from the Most Ancient Times. Hyderabad Publishers, 155–164.","Wikipedia contributors (2026). Umm Al Maa, citing Lorimer (1908) and Ferdinand (1993)."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"al-thaqab-fort","n":"Al Thaqab Fort","na":"قلعة الثقب","c":"archaeological","s":"Desert fort over a 35-foot well · 17th–19th century","lat":26.0325,"lng":51.1164,"p":"Late Islamic","dr":"Village from the 10th century · fort 17th – 19th century","el":"Well 35 ft (11 m) deep","ic":"well","era":"H","t0":1100,"t1":150,"te":"A fort to guard a well","ta":"قلعة تحرس بئرًا","se":"Four towers around a courtyard, built of coral and limestone where rainwater gathered in the wadi","sa":"أربعة أبراج حول فناء، بُنيت من المرجان والحجر الجيري حيث يتجمع ماء المطر في الوادي","desc":"A fort near the abandoned village of Ath Thaqab in Al Shamal, about 110 km from Doha and 4.8 km from Al Khuwayr. Its name comes from thaghab, the water that lies in channels after rain. The fort is square with three circular towers and one rectangular tower, a central courtyard with stairs, a bent entrance and buildings for storing and processing dates, built of rough coral and limestone blocks bonded with mud mortar; it is dated between the 17th and 19th centuries, while archaeological evidence suggests the village was inhabited from the 10th century. Lorimer in 1908 recorded a well about 35 feet (11 m) deep west of the fort whose good water was fetched by the people of Al Khuwayr. In July 1937 a force sent by Sheikh Abdullah Al Thani took the fort and confiscated 40 rifles from residents.","sig":"One of the chain of northern desert forts built to protect wells, the scarcest resource of the pearling-age villages.","ex":"Documented by J.G. Lorimer (1908); conserved and presented by Qatar Museums","disc":["Three round towers and one square tower","Well 35 ft deep used by neighbouring Al Khuwayr"],"refs":["Haroon, A. (9 Apr 2019). The historic Al Thaqab Fort. Gulf Times.","Wikipedia contributors (2026). Ath Thaqab, citing Lorimer, J.G. (1908) Gazetteer and Qatar Digital Library files."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"al-rekayat-fort","n":"Al Rekayat Fort","na":"قلعة الركيات","c":"archaeological","s":"Mid-18th-century well fort · ISESCO heritage list 2021","lat":26.0506,"lng":51.1358,"p":"Late Islamic","dr":"Mid-18th century (coins of 749–846 AD also found)","el":"28 × 22 m","ic":"wall","era":"H","t0":280,"t1":100,"te":"The fort named for wells","ta":"القلعة التي سُميت باسم الآبار","se":"A 28 by 22 m mud-and-limestone fort with a well inside, restored in 1988 and again in 2020–21","sa":"قلعة من الطين والحجر الجيري بأبعاد 28 × 22 م وبداخلها بئر، رُممت عام 1988 ثم في 2020–2021","desc":"A rectangular fort of mud and limestone, 28 × 22 m, on the road between Al Zubarah and Madinat Al Shamal about 110–115 km from Doha, with three rectangular corner towers and one cylindrical tower, narrow windowless rooms on three sides of a courtyard and a single entrance in the south wall. Its name refers to wells, and it was built to protect a freshwater well inside the fort for the surrounding village. Archaeological evidence suggests the mid-18th century, when Al Zubarah flourished, although coins of 749–846 AD were also found. First restored in 1988, it was conserved by Qatar Museums from October 2020 to September 2021, with a roof rebuilt in traditional danchal, basgil and manghrour, and in June 2021 it joined ISESCO's List of Heritage in the Islamic World.","sig":"A well-preserved example of the water-guarding desert forts of northern Qatar, now one of three Qatari sites on the ISESCO heritage list.","ex":"Qatar Museums Department of Architectural Conservation (A. al-Moslamani), 2020–2021; earlier restoration 1988","disc":["Freshwater well inside the walls","ISESCO Islamic World heritage list, June 2021"],"refs":["Adly, A. (26 Mar 2026). Archeological sites a testimony to the rich history of Qatar's human activities. Gulf Times.","QNA / The Peninsula (21 Nov 2022). Al Rekayat Fort documents Qatar's history and civilisation.","Gulf Times (22 Feb 2022). Qatar Museums restores historic Al Rakiyat Fort in north-east Qatar."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"jebel-nakhsh","n":"Jebel Nakhsh","na":"جبل النخش","c":"geological","s":"33 km Miocene ridge · type area of the Dam Formation","lat":24.874,"lng":50.9035,"p":"Dam Formation, Miocene","dr":"Miocene","el":"33 km long · c. 90 m high","ic":"cliff","era":"D","t0":23000000,"t1":5000000,"te":"A ridge of gypsum and stromatolites","ta":"ضلع من الجبس والستروماتوليت","se":"The highest ground in southern Qatar, three rock layers of a Miocene sea, mined for gypsum in 1948","sa":"أعلى أرض في جنوب قطر، ثلاث طبقات صخرية من بحر ميوسيني، استُخرج منها الجبس عام 1948","desc":"A ridge in southern Qatar running 33 km from south-east to north-west parallel to the west coast, rising to about 90 m. It exposes three members of the Miocene Dam Formation: the Salwa Member (about 27 m) at the base, the gypsum-rich Nakhsh Member (about 33 m), the only member with stromatolites, and the Abu Samrah Member (about 6 m) on top. Weathering of soft clays has collapsed the lower limestone into dens used by foxes and hyenas, caves first surveyed by Peter Whybrow in 1983–84. After a 1947 survey, British authorities had the ridge mined for gypsum, with a railway to Umm Bab; output reached 650 tons in the second half of 1949, though geologist Jacques Leblanc notes the mountain shows no clear signs of exploitation.","sig":"The best exposure of Qatar's Miocene sea-floor layers, and a landmark that shaped the geography of the south-west.","ex":"P. Whybrow (1983–84); J. Leblanc, QatarEnergy; P. Andrews (2008) on the hyaena den","disc":["Nakhsh Member 33 m thick, rich in gypsum","Hyaena dens in collapsed limestone"],"refs":["Leblanc, J. (2015). A Historical Account of the Stratigraphy of Qatar, Middle-East (1816 to 2015).","Andrews, P. (2008). Cetaceans from a possible striped hyaena den site in Qatar. Journal of Taphonomy 6(3–4).","Leblanc, J. (2009). A Fossil Hunting Guide to the Miocene of Qatar: Dam Formation."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"dukhan-sabkha","n":"Dukhan Sabkha","na":"سبخة دخان","c":"geological","s":"Inland salt flat below sea level · Holocene evaporites","lat":25.32,"lng":50.85,"p":"Holocene","dr":"Last c. 5,000 years","el":"107 km² basin · 1–2 m below sea level","ic":"salt","era":"D","t0":5000,"t1":0,"te":"A sea that dried into salt","ta":"بحر جفّ فصار ملحًا","se":"A 107 km² hollow between the Dukhan anticline and the Qatar Arch where gypsum, halite and anhydrite still crystallise","sa":"منخفض مساحته 107 كم² بين طيّة دخان المحدبة وقوس قطر، ما يزال يتبلور فيه الجبس والهاليت والأنهيدريت","desc":"An inland sabkha on the west coast south of Dukhan, filling a synclinal depression between the Dukhan anticline to the west and the Qatar Arch to the east. Holocene sediments cover a structural low of 107 km², mostly 1–2 m below sea level, 4–6 m thick and up to 12 m, over Eocene carbonates; a 12 km² salt flat in the north-east is the youngest lake deposit. Gypsum is the dominant mineral (20–30%), with halite 8–11% and anhydrite about 5%, and prismatic gypsum up to 7 m thick records a saline lake at the centre. Sea-level reconstruction shows an early Holocene marine embayment flooding from the north that became ever more restricted into evaporite lakes; Qatar University work dates the sabkha to the last 5,000 years. Qatar has 78 inland sabkha patches covering about 205 km² and 42 coastal sabkhas totalling 701 km².","sig":"A natural laboratory for how evaporites form, studied by Bristol and ExxonMobil Research Qatar as a model for ancient salt-bearing rocks.","ex":"University of Bristol and ExxonMobil Research Qatar (Didi-Ooi, Whitaker, Jameson, Strohmenger), 2014–2015; Qatar University Science Journal","disc":["Gypsum beds up to 7 m thick","Water table 0.5–1 m down, rising and falling 40 cm a year"],"refs":["Didi-Ooi, S.M., Whitaker, F., Simmonds, J., Jameson, J. & Strohmenger, C.J. (2015). Evaporite diagenesis models of a continental sabkha system: insights from the Holocene at Dukhan, Qatar. AAPG ACE, Denver.","Qatar University Science Journal. Geologic evolution of the Dukhan salt flats in western Qatar Peninsula, Arabian Gulf.","James, B. (5 Mar 2011). Call to highlight potential of hyper-saline coastal flats. Gulf Times."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"mesaieed-sabkha","n":"Mesaieed Coastal Sabkha","na":"سبخة مسيعيد","c":"geological","s":"Largest coastal salt flat in Qatar · 55 km long","lat":24.85,"lng":51.45,"p":"Holocene","dr":"Last c. 6,000 years","el":"55 km long · 4–14 km wide","ic":"salt","era":"D","t0":6000,"t1":0,"te":"A lagoon filled with sand","ta":"بحيرة شاطئية ملأتها الرمال","se":"A former lagoon south of Mesaieed, now a gypsum-cemented plain that grew seaward over six thousand years","sa":"بحيرة شاطئية قديمة جنوب مسيعيد صارت سهلًا متماسكًا بالجبس تقدّم نحو البحر على مدى ستة آلاف عام","desc":"The largest coastal sabkha in Qatar, on the east coast just south of Mesaieed, roughly 55 km long and 4–14 km wide, formed from a former lagoon that filled with wind-blown siliciclastic sand. Bristol and ExxonMobil Research Qatar geologists describe it as a prograding coastal plain 4–6 km wide whose Holocene wedge is 3–6 m thick, reaching about 15 m at the coast, over Eocene limestones and dolomites. Gypsum is the main diagenetic mineral, 20–50% of the sediment over several km², with calcite, dolomite, anhydrite and halite minor; gypsum cement is pervasive in the inland part (about 6,000 years old), restricted to crusts in the centre (about 4,000 years) and largely absent at the coast (about 2,000 years). Evaporative pumping of groundwater from the Eocene aquifer is the main source of salts, and the gypsum helps stabilise a surface that vehicles can cross when dry.","sig":"A textbook example of a prograding coastal sabkha, showing how a shoreline moved seaward and cemented itself with gypsum.","ex":"University of Bristol and ExxonMobil Research Qatar (Didi-Ooi, Whitaker, Jameson, Strohmenger), 2014; UNESCO Sabkha Ecosystems volume (B. Boer)","disc":["Gypsum 20–50% of the sediment","Three age zones: 6,000, 4,000 and 2,000 years"],"refs":["Didi-Ooi, S.M., Whitaker, F., Jameson, J. & Strohmenger, C.J. (2014). Mesaieed sabkha, southeast Qatar: evaporite diagenesis in a prograding coastal plain. AAPG ACE, Houston.","James, B. (5 Mar 2011). Call to highlight potential of hyper-saline coastal flats. Gulf Times (on the Sabkha Ecosystems series)."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"dahl-al-hamam","n":"Dahl Al Hamam","na":"دحل الحمام","c":"geological","s":"Collapse sinkhole in a Doha park · tidal pool inside","lat":25.3344,"lng":51.4805,"p":"Karst in Eocene Rus and Dammam formations","dr":"Middle Pleistocene to present","el":"60 m long · entrance 12 m wide, 9 m high","ic":"sinkhole","era":"D","t0":700000,"t1":0,"te":"A cave in the middle of the city","ta":"كهف في قلب المدينة","se":"A collapse doline whose brackish pool rises and falls 30 cm with the tide, one of more than 9,700 depressions in Qatar","sa":"حفرة انهيارية ترتفع بركتها المالحة وتنخفض 30 سم مع المد والجزر، واحدة من أكثر من 9,700 منخفض في قطر","desc":"A collapse doline, or sinkhole, fenced in the north-east corner of Dahl Al Hamam Park in central Doha, about 60 m long with a portal 12 m wide and 9 m high and a staircase of about 20 steps down the cliff. Brackish water at its sides rises and falls about 30 cm with the tides, suggesting a connection to the sea; divers explored underwater passages behind the pool in 2016, and a 2016 gravity survey by Howari, Aldouri and Sadiq found a strong negative anomaly marking water-filled caves linked to the Arabian Gulf. Lorimer in 1908 called it Dahail and noted a rocky pool of good water. It belongs to a karst landscape of more than 9,700 depressions in Qatar, formed by subsurface dissolution of limestone, dolomite, gypsum and anhydrite of the Eocene Rus and Dammam formations under wet Middle Pleistocene conditions, followed by subsidence. Pigeons nesting inside gave the cave, and the district, its name.","sig":"The most accessible of Qatar's dahl caves, and a reminder that the capital stands on dissolving Eocene rock.","ex":"Sadiq & Nasir (2002) karst study; Howari, Aldouri & Sadiq (2016) gravity survey; Doha municipality park","disc":["Pool that moves with the tide","More than 9,700 karst depressions in Qatar"],"refs":["Sadiq, A.M. & Nasir, S.J. (2002). Middle Pleistocene karst evolution in the State of Qatar, Arabian Gulf. Journal of Cave and Karst Studies 64(2), 132–139.","Howari, F.M., Aldouri, R. & Sadiq, A. (2016). Gravity investigations of recent sinkholes and karst pits of Dahal Al-Hamam, State of Qatar. Environmental Earth Sciences 75(5), 440.","Showcaves.com. Dahl Al Hamam (2025)."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"al-shahaniya-oryx-reserve","n":"Al Shahaniya Oryx Reserve","na":"محمية الشحانية","c":"ecology","s":"Qatar's first Arabian oryx sanctuary · 1979","lat":25.37,"lng":51.22,"p":"Modern conservation","dr":"1979 – present","el":"12 km² · 45 km from Doha","ic":"gazelle","era":"N","t0":47,"t1":0,"te":"Where the white oryx came back","ta":"حيث عاد المها الأبيض","se":"A 12 km² reserve founded in 1979 for the national animal, declared extinct in the wild in 1972","sa":"محمية مساحتها 12 كم² أُسست عام 1979 للحيوان الوطني الذي أُعلن انقراضه في البرية عام 1972","desc":"A 12 km² reserve at Al Shahaniya, about 45 km from Doha, designated in 1979 as a sanctuary for the Arabian oryx and one of the first nature reserves established by the State. The oryx were brought from Muaither farm by Sheikh Abdul Rahman bin Saud Al Thani, and Sheikh Jassim bin Hamad Al Thani was the first to give the animals special attention there; the reserve also holds a pen for North African red-necked ostriches. The species had been declared extinct in the wild in October 1972 after poachers killed the last herd in Oman; Sheikh Faisal bin Qassim Al Thani's 1967 expedition caught five animals in Oman that founded Qatar's Royal Herd. The IUCN moved the oryx from Endangered to Vulnerable in 2011, and the animals have since spread to more than 10 protected areas in Qatar, with up to 10,000 wild and semi-wild oryx estimated in the country.","sig":"The starting point of Qatar's oryx recovery, one of the Gulf's most successful returns of an animal from extinction in the wild.","ex":"Ministry of Environment and Climate Change, Natural Reserves Department; Qatari royal herds since 1967","disc":["Founded 1979, 12 km²","Oryx now in more than 10 protected areas"],"refs":["Qatar News Agency (15 Apr 2025). Qatar's nature reserves: promoting ecosystem sustainability and wildlife preservation.","eNature Qatar (Ministry of Environment and Climate Change). Al Sheehaniya Reserve.","Payne, S. (5 Jan 2025). The Arabian oryx: how Qatar's national animal came back from the dead. BBC Discover Wildlife."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"al-mashabiya-reserve","n":"Al Mashabiya Reserve","na":"محمية المسحبية","c":"ecology","s":"54 km² oryx and gazelle reserve in the south-west · 1997","lat":24.72,"lng":50.95,"p":"Modern conservation","dr":"1997 – present","el":"c. 54 km² · c. 1,000 oryx","ic":"gazelle","era":"N","t0":29,"t1":0,"te":"A thousand oryx among the dunes","ta":"ألف مها بين الكثبان","se":"Sandy plains south-east of Abu Samra where oryx and reem gazelles are bred for release","sa":"سهول رملية جنوب شرق أبو سمرة تُربّى فيها المها وغزلان الريم لإطلاقها","desc":"A reserve of about 54 km² in south-west Qatar, south-east of Abu Samra, officially opened in 1997 to resettle the Arabian oryx, the reem (sand) gazelle and other endangered animals. Its terrain is sandy plains with hills and valleys where natural grasses, seasonal herbs and wild trees grow among the dunes. According to its manager Mohammed Nasser Al Henzab, quoted in January 2025, the reserve holds around 1,000 oryx. It is one of 12 terrestrial and marine reserves that, with Umm Al Sheif approved at the end of 2024, cover about 25.73% of Qatar's territory, with a national goal of 30% by 2030 under the 2004 wildlife protection decree.","sig":"The largest single oryx herd reported in Qatar, and the breeding base for the country's gazelle and oryx reintroductions.","ex":"Ministry of Environment and Climate Change, Natural Reserves Department (S.H. Al Kuwari); reserve manager M.N. Al Henzab","disc":["About 1,000 oryx reported in 2025","Reserves cover 25.73% of Qatar"],"refs":["Qatar News Agency (15 Apr 2025). Qatar's nature reserves: promoting ecosystem sustainability and wildlife preservation.","eNature Qatar (Ministry of Environment and Climate Change). Al Mashabiya Reserve.","Payne, S. (5 Jan 2025). The Arabian oryx: how Qatar's national animal came back from the dead. BBC Discover Wildlife."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"irkaya-farm","n":"Irkaya Farm and Reserve","na":"محمية مزرعة إركية","c":"ecology","s":"Qatar's richest birding site · 238 species recorded","lat":25.01,"lng":51.18,"p":"Modern farmland oasis","dr":"Late 20th century – present","el":"238 of Qatar's 315 bird species","ic":"bird","era":"N","t0":50,"t1":0,"te":"Fodder fields full of birds","ta":"حقول علف تعجّ بالطيور","se":"Irrigated pivot fields on the Abu Samra road where 76% of all birds ever recorded in Qatar have been seen","sa":"حقول محورية مروية على طريق أبو سمرة شوهد فيها 76% من كل الطيور المسجلة في قطر","desc":"A fodder farm off the Abu Samra Road about 50 km west of Doha, in the south-central plain, watered by central-pivot irrigation on a flat sandy plain. By 2014, when a book on its fauna and flora was launched at the Ministry of Environment, 238 of the 315 bird species recorded in Qatar (76%) had been seen at Irkaya, more than anywhere else in the country, including 15 globally threatened species such as the sociable lapwing, and many of Qatar's 59 breeding species nest there. Native plants near the pivot run-off grow taller with larger leaves than in the open desert; birders report close to 250 species and a southern farm of roughly 15 km² with lagoons, reed beds and grassland. The Ministry now lists Erkia as a nature reserve on the migratory route of birds and insects, with a farm growing animal feed inside it.","sig":"Proof that a single irrigated farm can become the most important stopover for migratory birds on the peninsula.","ex":"Ministry of Environment and Climate Change (Erkia Reserve); Qatar birdwatchers who compiled the 2014 Irkaya book","disc":["15 globally threatened bird species recorded","Sociable lapwing, one of the world's rarest waders"],"refs":["The Peninsula (10 Mar 2014). Fauna, flora of Irkaya farm.","Qatar News Agency (15 Apr 2025). Qatar's nature reserves: promoting ecosystem sustainability and wildlife preservation (Erkia Reserve).","Birdingplaces.eu. Irakhiya Farm, Qatar (site guide)."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"abu-nakhla-wetland","n":"Abu Nakhla Wetland","na":"بحيرة أبو نخلة","c":"ecology","s":"Artificial wetland of 9 km² south of Doha · flamingo flocks","lat":25.19,"lng":51.42,"p":"Modern","dr":"1982 – present","el":"9 km² · 12 km from Doha","ic":"bird","era":"N","t0":44,"t1":0,"te":"A lake made of waste water","ta":"بحيرة من ماء الصرف المعالج","se":"A treated-sewage reservoir since 1982 that became Qatar's biggest wetland and a flamingo haunt, then faced draining","sa":"خزان لمياه الصرف المعالجة منذ عام 1982 صار أكبر أرض رطبة في قطر ومقصدًا للنحام، ثم واجه التجفيف","desc":"An artificial wetland with natural topography about 12 km south of Doha near Abu Nakhla village, created in 1982 as a reservoir for treated sewage effluent from the Doha-South and Doha-West treatment stations, spread over nine square kilometres. At least 260 bird species have been recorded, with about 150 greater flamingos counted, frogs and fish in the pond, reeds and cat's-tail around it, and about 10% of Qatar's documented plant species in the area; the pond sits on the Simsima/Umm Bab and Midra Shale members of the Eocene Dammam Formation over a shallow aquifer, and a 2014–2015 study found low bacterial contamination and acceptable heavy-metal levels. In March 2014 the Public Works Authority Ashghal, under its president Nasser bin Ali al-Mawlawi, announced the lagoon would be drained by the end of that year; Qatar University's Anna Grichting Solder, who studied the site with her students, and Saif al-Hajri of Friends of the Environment argued in 2015 for an ecological wetland reserve with bio-drainage and bioremediation, noting the Ramsar secretariat and BirdLife International were ready to support a Ramsar listing, as Abu Dhabi's Al Wathba had received in 2013.","sig":"The largest wetland in Qatar, an accidental ecosystem that raised the question of what a desert city owes the birds it attracted.","ex":"Qatar University Department of Architecture and Urban Planning (A. Grichting Solder); Friends of the Environment (S. al-Hajri)","disc":["At least 260 bird species, c. 150 greater flamingos","Drainage plan announced in 2014"],"refs":["Holla, A. (25 Aug 2015). A case for Qatar's biggest wetland. Gulf Times.","Gulf Times (6 Mar 2014). Abu Nakhla lagoon to be drained by end of this year.","Elobaid, E.A., Sadooni, F.N., Lebbe, L. et al. (2018). Environmental evaluation, risk assessment and management options of Abu Nakhla treated sewage effluent unlined pond. Qatar Foundation Annual Research Conference Proceedings."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"al-aaliya-island","n":"Al Aaliya Island","na":"جزيرة العالية","c":"ecology","s":"Seabird colony island off Doha · Important Bird Area","lat":25.4064,"lng":51.5656,"p":"Modern","dr":"Present","el":"2 km sandspit at low tide","ic":"island","era":"N","t0":100,"t1":0,"te":"Cormorants within sight of the towers","ta":"غاقات على مرمى من الأبراج","se":"A low coral-reef island north of The Pearl where Socotra cormorants and white-cheeked terns breed","sa":"جزيرة مرجانية منخفضة شمال اللؤلؤة يتكاثر فيها غاق سقطرى والخرشنة بيضاء الخد","desc":"A small, low-lying island off Al Daayen municipality north of The Pearl, 3.5 miles north of Al Safliya Island, with a brown surface and a small peak at its east end that once guided ships into Al Bidda harbour. Unlike Halul, which rests on ancient igneous and metamorphic rock, it is built of coral-reef structures, with loose weathered limestone, scattered Limonium and Zygophyllum bushes, a 2 km sandspit extending south at low tide and broad intertidal flats and coral reefs around it. BirdLife International designated it an Important Bird Area for its breeding colonies of Socotra cormorants and white-cheeked terns. The artificial island of The Pearl, built in the late 2000s, now lies between Al Aaliya and Al Safliya.","sig":"A breeding colony of the Gulf-endemic Socotra cormorant a few kilometres from Doha's skyline.","ex":"BirdLife International (Important Bird Area / Key Biodiversity Area); Centre for GIS of Qatar","disc":["Socotra cormorant and white-cheeked tern colonies","Coral-reef island, not bedrock"],"refs":["BirdLife International (2024). Important Bird Area factsheet: Al-Aliyah island, Qatar.","Wikipedia contributors (2026). Al Aaliya Island, citing the British Hydrographic Department Gulf Pilot (1890) and BirdLife Data Zone (2024)."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"umm-tais-national-park","n":"Umm Tais National Park","na":"حديقة أم تيس الوطنية","c":"ecology","s":"Island park at Qatar's northern tip · hawksbill nesting","lat":26.12,"lng":51.3,"p":"Modern conservation","dr":"2006 – present","el":"One of 8 main turtle nesting sites · 219 turtles nested in 2025","ic":"mangrove","era":"N","t0":20,"t1":0,"te":"Mangroves at the top of the map","ta":"قرم في أقصى الشمال","se":"Sand bars, islets and mangroves on an uninhabited island declared a national park in 2006","sa":"حواجز رملية وجزر صغيرة وقرم على جزيرة غير مأهولة أُعلنت حديقة وطنية عام 2006","desc":"A national park on an uninhabited island at the northern tip of Qatar, within 6.4 km of Ar Ruwais, established in 2006 during the 15th Asian Games. It has sand bars and small islets with mangroves, is a stopover for migratory birds on the Afro-Eurasian flyway and a nursery ground for fish and crustaceans, with dugong sightings in surrounding waters, and is one of the main hawksbill sea turtle nesting sites in the country. In the 2025 season, from 31 March to the end of July, the Ministry of Environment and Climate Change with QatarEnergy and Qatar University's Environmental Science Center recorded 219 nesting turtles across eight main sites including Umm Tais, Fuwairit, Ras Rakan, Ras Laffan and Al Ghariya, and released 8,213 hatchlings, bringing the project's five-year total to more than 60,000. The ruins of the old village of Al Mafjar lie nearby.","sig":"The northernmost protected area of the peninsula, joining mangroves, seabirds and hawksbill turtles in one small island.","ex":"Ministry of Environment and Climate Change, Wildlife Development Department; QatarEnergy; Qatar University Environmental Science Center","disc":["National park since 2006","8,213 hawksbill hatchlings released nationwide in 2025"],"refs":["Qatar News Agency (2 Aug 2025). MoECC announces conclusion of 2025 nesting season for hawksbill sea turtles.","Wikipedia contributors (2026). Umm Tais National Park, citing Marhaba (2015) and Orr, T. (2008) Qatar (Cultures of the World).","Plizio Visual Lab. Umm Tais National Park, Qatar."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"spec-gypsum-karst","n":"One Gypsum, Two Landscapes","c":"speculative","s":"Cross-category hypothesis","lat":25.38,"lng":51.03,"p":"Eocene rock, Middle Pleistocene to present karst","dr":"c. 2.5 million years ago – today (test window)","el":"","desc":"A connection between the Eocene evaporite layers of Qatar and two very different landscapes. At Zekreet, a 2 m limestone cap of the Dammam Formation protects 10 m of soft marl, and wind and salt carve mesas and mushroom rocks beside gypsum plateaux. Forty kilometres to the south-east, Dahl Al Misfir is a 40 m sinkhole lined with fibrous gypsum crystals derived from the Lower Dammam and Rus formations, part of a karst of more than 9,700 depressions that formed under wet Middle Pleistocene conditions. The idea: the same gypsum-bearing layers control both. Where they lie at the surface they feed the salt weathering that shapes Zekreet; where they lie a few metres down, rainwater dissolves them, the roof collapses, and the dissolved gypsum re-grows as the glowing fibres of the dahl.","sig":"If true, a single map of gypsum thickness and depth in the Rus and Lower Dammam formations would predict both where the peninsula is prone to sinkholes, a hazard for roads and buildings, and where its most striking scenery will keep forming.","ex":"","conf":"Untested AI speculation (connection between published records)","ev":"Zekreet Peninsula: Abarug member of the Dammam Formation, 2 m limestone over 10 m marl, with gypsum plateaux (Leblanc 2008). Dahl Al Misfir: 40 m deep, entrance 12 × 4.5 m, fibrous gypsum from the Lower Dammam and Rus formations, Middle Pleistocene karst (Sadiq & Nasir 2002). Dahl Al Hamam: collapse doline in the Rus and Dammam formations, one of more than 9,700 depressions formed by dissolution of limestone, dolomite, gypsum and anhydrite (Sadiq & Nasir 2002; Howari et al. 2016). Dukhan Sabkha: gypsum 20–30% of Holocene sediment, 7 m prismatic gypsum beds, showing how readily gypsum dissolves and re-precipitates in this climate (Didi-Ooi et al. 2015). Distances: Zekreet to Dahl Al Misfir about 45 km; Dahl Al Misfir to Dahl Al Hamam about 30 km.","an":"In the Western Ukraine, the largest gypsum caves on Earth (Optymistychna and its neighbours) formed by dissolution of a single Miocene gypsum bed 10–30 m thick lying under thin cover, and the pattern of collapse dolines at the surface follows that bed; Klimchouk (1996) showed that the depth of the gypsum below the water table, not rainfall alone, decides where caves and collapses form.","val":"Log gypsum thickness and depth below surface in boreholes and quarry faces along a transect from Zekreet to Dahl Al Misfir and Dahl Al Hamam; map the 9,700 karst depressions against that surface; analyse sulphur and oxygen isotopes of the Dahl Al Misfir fibres and of Rus gypsum to test whether one came from the other; date the fibres (U-series) and compare with the Middle Pleistocene wet phases.","disc":["Rival explanation 1: the dahl caves formed by dissolution of limestone and dolomite alone, and the gypsum fibres grew later from evaporating groundwater unrelated to the Rus layers.","Rival explanation 2: Zekreet’s mesas are a product of the hard-cap-over-soft-marl structure and wind only, and gypsum plays no part in their shaping.","Rival explanation 3: sinkhole distribution follows fractures and the Qatar Arch rather than gypsum thickness."],"refs":["Leblanc, J. (2008). A fossil hunting guide to the Tertiary formations of Qatar.","Sadiq, A. M. & Nasir, S. J. (2002). Middle Pleistocene karst evolution in the State of Qatar, Arabian Gulf. Journal of Cave and Karst Studies 64(2), 132–139.","Howari, F.M., Aldouri, R. & Sadiq, A. (2016). Gravity investigations of recent sinkholes and karst pits of Dahal Al-Hamam, State of Qatar. Environmental Earth Sciences 75(5), 440.","Didi-Ooi, S.M., Whitaker, F., Simmonds, J., Jameson, J. & Strohmenger, C.J. (2015). Evaporite diagenesis models of a continental sabkha system: insights from the Holocene at Dukhan, Qatar. AAPG ACE, Denver.","Klimchouk, A. (1996). Gypsum karst in the Western Ukraine. International Journal of Speleology 25(3–4), 263–278."],"ic":"spark","era":"?","te":"Did the Same Salt Carve the Mushrooms and Light the Cave?","ta":"هل نحت الملح نفسه الفطر وأضاء الكهف؟","se":"The gypsum that makes Dahl Al Misfir glow may be the same salt that sculpts the mushroom rocks of Zekreet: one layer, two landscapes. Nobody has tested this yet.","sa":"قد يكون الجبس الذي يجعل دحل المسفر يتوهج هو الملح نفسه الذي ينحت صخور الفطر في زكريت: طبقة واحدة، ومنظران. لم يختبر أحد ذلك بعد.","na":"جبس واحد، منظران","t0":2580000,"t1":0,"spec":{"bas":"link","key":"gypsum","sty":"ai-gypsum","spot":{"en":"Born from a coincidence of rock names on this map. The Zekreet record speaks of gypsum plateaux in the Dammam Formation; the Dahl Al Misfir record says its glowing crystals come from the Lower Dammam and Rus formations; the Dahl Al Hamam record counts more than 9,700 depressions dissolved out of the same rocks. Three records, one mineral, 45 km apart.","ar":"وُلدت الفكرة من تطابق أسماء الصخور على هذه الخريطة. يذكر سجل زكريت هضابًا جبسية في تكوين الدمام؛ ويقول سجل دحل المسفر إن بلوراته المتوهجة جاءت من تكويني الدمام الأدنى والرس؛ ويعدّ سجل دحل الحمام أكثر من 9,700 منخفض ذاب من الصخور نفسها. ثلاثة سجلات، ومعدن واحد، تفصلها 45 كم."},"lk":[["zekreet-mesas","Hard cap over soft marl, beside gypsum plateaux","غطاء صلب فوق مارل طري، بجوار هضاب جبسية"],["dahl-al-misfir","Fibrous gypsum from the Rus and Lower Dammam formations","جبس ليفي من تكويني الرس والدمام الأدنى"],["dahl-al-hamam","One of more than 9,700 dissolution depressions","واحد من أكثر من 9,700 منخفض إذابة"],["dukhan-sabkha","How fast gypsum dissolves and re-grows in this climate","كم يذوب الجبس ويتبلور من جديد بسرعة في هذا المناخ"]],"pred":{"en":"Karst depressions will cluster where the Rus and Lower Dammam gypsum lies within a few metres of the surface and within reach of the water table, and the isotopes of the Dahl Al Misfir fibres will match that gypsum, not seawater or sabkha brine.","ar":"ستتجمع المنخفضات الكارستية حيث يقع جبس الرس والدمام الأدنى على بُعد أمتار قليلة من السطح وفي متناول منسوب المياه الجوفية، وستطابق نظائر ألياف دحل المسفر ذلك الجبس، لا ماء البحر ولا محلول السبخة."},"test":{"en":"Map gypsum depth and thickness along a Zekreet to Doha transect, overlay the 9,700 depressions, and compare sulphur and oxygen isotopes of the cave fibres with the Rus gypsum.","ar":"رسم عمق الجبس وسمكه على مقطع من زكريت إلى الدوحة، ووضع المنخفضات التسعة آلاف وسبعمئة فوقه، ومقارنة نظائر الكبريت والأكسجين في ألياف الكهف مع جبس الرس."},"wrong":{"en":"If depressions are just as common where the gypsum is absent or deeply buried, or if the cave fibres carry a brine or seawater signature, the single-gypsum idea fails.","ar":"إذا كانت المنخفضات شائعة بالقدر نفسه حيث يغيب الجبس أو يُدفن عميقًا، أو إذا حملت ألياف الكهف بصمة محلول ملحي أو ماء بحر، تسقط فكرة الجبس الواحد."}}},{"id":"spec-ubaid-water","n":"Camps by the Freshwater Hollows","c":"speculative","s":"Cross-category hypothesis","lat":25.6,"lng":50.92,"p":"Neolithic (Ubaid period)","dr":"c. 7,500 – 4,500 years ago (test window)","el":"","desc":"A connection between Qatar’s oldest camps and its karst. Al Da’asa on the west coast, with nearly 60 hearths and painted Ubaid pottery, was a seasonal camp that the same group returned to again and again; Wadi Debayan on the north-west coast was occupied from about 7,500 years ago. Both lie on a peninsula with more than 9,700 karst depressions, the dahl and rawdat that collect rain: at Barzan a rawda was worth guarding with towers, at Umm Al Maa the Mleiha depression had wells good enough to be named for them, and Dahl Al Hamam held a pool of good water in 1908. The idea: the Neolithic camps were pinned to places where a freshwater hollow lay within a day’s walk of a fishing shore, and the map of such hollows is the map of where the next Ubaid sites will be found.","sig":"If true, it would explain why people from the Ubaid world could live on a nearly rainless peninsula, would give archaeologists a way to predict undiscovered Neolithic sites from geology alone, and would show that Qatar’s karst has shaped human life since the first campfires.","ex":"","conf":"Untested AI speculation (connection between published records)","ev":"Al Da’asa: nearly 60 hearths and tent postholes, a camp returned to repeatedly, 7,500–6,000 years ago (Danish expedition 1961; de Cardi 1978). Wadi Debayan: occupation from about 7,500 years ago on the north-west coast (QNHER project 2010–2013). Karst: more than 9,700 depressions formed under wet Middle Pleistocene conditions (Sadiq & Nasir 2002), so they already existed when the camps were made. Water in the hollows: Umm Al Maa, freshwater wells in the 25 km² Mleiha depression, about 45 km from Al Da’asa; Barzan rawda collecting rainwater; Dahl Al Hamam, a rocky pool of good water in 1908. Al Da’asa lies about 15 km from the Dukhan Sabkha, which was a marine embayment in the early Holocene.","an":"Along the Jubbah palaeolake in the Nefud Desert of northern Saudi Arabia, Petraglia and colleagues (2012) showed that Palaeolithic and later occupations cluster around a basin that held fresh water in wetter phases: the lake, not the surrounding sand, decided where people lived, and dating the lake sediments dated the people.","val":"Core the floor of rawdat and dahl within 10 km of Al Da’asa and Wadi Debayan for Holocene freshwater sediments (pond marls, freshwater snails, pollen) and date them; survey the rims of the hollows for flint scatters and Ubaid sherds; compare the density of known Neolithic finds within 5 km of a water-holding depression against the density elsewhere on the same coast; analyse strontium isotopes in Al Da’asa shells and bone to tell local from distant water.","disc":["Rival explanation 1: the camps were placed only for the sea, beside fish, shellfish and the sheltered embayment that later became the Dukhan Sabkha, and water was carried or dug from the beach.","Rival explanation 2: the Neolithic climate was wet enough that rain and shallow wells gave water almost everywhere, so the hollows gave no advantage.","Rival explanation 3: the clustering is an artefact of where archaeologists have looked, mainly near modern roads, wells and oil-camp villages."],"refs":["Wikipedia contributors (2026). Al Da'asa, citing Rice, M. (1994) The Archaeology of the Arabian Gulf and Abdul Nayeem, M. (1998) Qatar Prehistory and Protohistory.","Casey, P. & Vine, P. (1991). The Heritage of Qatar. Immel.","Cuttler, R. et al. Qatar National Historic Environment Record project reports, University of Birmingham.","Sadiq, A. M. & Nasir, S. J. (2002). Middle Pleistocene karst evolution in the State of Qatar, Arabian Gulf. Journal of Cave and Karst Studies 64(2), 132–139.","Wikipedia contributors (2026). Umm Al Maa, citing Lorimer (1908) and Ferdinand (1993).","Petraglia, M.D., Alsharekh, A., Breeze, P., Clarkson, C., Crassard, R., Drake, N.A., Groucutt, H.S., Jennings, R., Parker, A.G., Parton, A., Roberts, R.G., Shipton, C., Matheson, C., al-Omari, A. & Veall, M.-A. (2012). Hominin dispersal into the Nefud Desert and Middle Palaeolithic settlement along the Jubbah palaeolake, northern Arabia. PLoS ONE 7(11), e49840."],"ic":"spark","era":"?","te":"Did the Dahl and Rawdat Decide Where the First Qataris Camped?","ta":"هل حددت الدحول والروضات أين خيّم أوائل القطريين؟","se":"The first people of Qatar may have chosen their campsites by the hollows that held rain, and the 9,700 dahl and rawdat of the peninsula might be a treasure map of lost Neolithic camps. Nobody has tested this yet.","sa":"ربما اختار أوائل سكان قطر مواضع مخيماتهم بحسب المنخفضات التي تحفظ ماء المطر، وقد تكون دحول شبه الجزيرة وروضاتها التسعة آلاف وسبعمئة خريطة كنز لمخيمات نيوليثية مفقودة. لم يختبر أحد ذلك بعد.","na":"مخيمات عند منخفضات الماء العذب","t0":7500,"t1":4500,"spec":{"bas":"link","key":"rawda","sty":"ai-rawda","spot":{"en":"Born from a question on this map. Al Da’asa’s record says a group returned to the same spot for centuries and lit nearly 60 hearths there, on a coast with almost no rain. Other records show what water this peninsula does hold: wells in the Umm Al Maa depression, a guarded rawda at Barzan, a pool in Dahl Al Hamam. Putting the camps and the hollows on one map raised the idea.","ar":"وُلدت الفكرة من سؤال على هذه الخريطة. يقول سجل الدعاسة إن جماعة عادت إلى الموضع نفسه قرونًا وأشعلت فيه نحو ستين موقدًا، على ساحل يكاد يخلو من المطر. وتبيّن سجلات أخرى أي ماء تحفظه شبه الجزيرة: آبار في منخفض أم الماء، وروضة محروسة في برزان، وبركة في دحل الحمام. ووضع المخيمات والمنخفضات على خريطة واحدة أثار الفكرة."},"lk":[["al-daasa","Nearly 60 hearths of a camp people kept coming back to","نحو ستين موقدًا لمخيم ظل الناس يعودون إليه"],["wadi-debayan","Occupied from about 7,500 years ago","مسكون منذ نحو 7,500 عام"],["umm-al-maa","A depression named for its freshwater wells","منخفض سُمّي باسم آباره العذبة"],["dahl-al-hamam","One of 9,700 hollows, with a pool of good water in 1908","واحد من 9,700 منخفض، فيه بركة ماء طيب عام 1908"]],"pred":{"en":"Cores from hollows near Al Da’asa and Wadi Debayan will hold freshwater pond sediments dated to 7,500–4,500 years ago, and Neolithic flint and Ubaid sherds will be several times denser within 5 km of such hollows than along the rest of the coast.","ar":"ستحوي العيّنات اللبية من المنخفضات قرب الدعاسة ووادي دبيان رواسب برك عذبة مؤرخة بين 7,500 و4,500 عام مضت، وستكون كثافة الصوان النيوليثي والكسر العبيدية أعلى عدة مرات في نطاق 5 كم من تلك المنخفضات منها على بقية الساحل."},"test":{"en":"Core and date the hollows, survey their rims for flint and pottery, and compare find density near water-holding depressions with the rest of the coast.","ar":"أخذ عيّنات لبية من المنخفضات وتأريخها، ومسح حوافها بحثًا عن الصوان والفخار، ومقارنة كثافة اللقى قرب المنخفضات الحافظة للماء مع بقية الساحل."},"wrong":{"en":"If the hollows near the camps were dry in the Neolithic, or if Ubaid finds are spread evenly along the coast with no regard to them, the idea fails.","ar":"إذا كانت المنخفضات قرب المخيمات جافة في العصر النيوليثي، أو إذا انتشرت اللقى العبيدية بالتساوي على الساحل من دون صلة بها، تسقط الفكرة."}}},{"id":"spec-dune-lagoon","n":"The Lagoon the Dunes Are Filling","c":"speculative","s":"Cross-category hypothesis","lat":24.82,"lng":51.42,"p":"Holocene","dr":"c. 6,000 years ago – today (test window)","el":"","desc":"A connection between three records on the south-east coast. The Mesaieed Coastal Sabkha, 55 km long, is a former lagoon that filled with wind-blown sand and cemented itself with gypsum: pervasive inland (about 6,000 years old), crusts in the centre (4,000 years), almost none at the coast (2,000 years). Just south, barchan and parabolic dunes with 0–1% vegetation march south-east on the shamal and spill straight into the tidal lagoon of Khor Al Adaid, where sabkhas already prograde seaward. The idea: Khor Al Adaid is a Mesaieed sabkha at an earlier stage, and the dune field, whose sand supply is nearly exhausted, is the hourglass that sets how long the Inland Sea has left as open water.","sig":"If true, the fate of a landscape on UNESCO’s Tentative List, and of the dugongs, turtles, corals and seagrass that live in it, could be forecast from a sand budget, and decisions about sand mining, fencing or planting on the dunes would become decisions about the lagoon.","ex":"","conf":"Untested AI speculation (connection between published records)","ev":"Mesaieed Sabkha: a former lagoon filled with wind-blown siliciclastic sand, prograding coastal plain 4–6 km wide, gypsum cement zoned at 6,000, 4,000 and 2,000 years (Didi-Ooi et al. 2014). Mesaieed dunes: driven by the north-north-westerly shamal toward the Inland Sea, vegetation 0–1%, sand supply limited to exhausted, possibly gone in 500–1,500 years (James 2011; Al-Awah & Matter 2023). Khor Al Adaid: dunes reach and spill into the sea, inland and coastal sabkhas prograde seaward under the shamal, salt hummocks record Holocene sea-level change, 10 km tidal channel (UNESCO Tentative List 2008). The three records lie in a 25 km line from north to south along the same wind.","an":"On the coast of the United Arab Emirates, Kirkham (1998) showed that Pleistocene carbonate seif dunes, drowned and reworked by the Holocene sea, set the shape of today’s lagoons and barrier islands, and that sabkhas there formed where wind-blown sand and tidal flats filled sheltered water: the same sequence, lagoon to filled flat, is written in the rocks along the southern shore of the Arabian Gulf.","val":"Core the floor of Khor Al Adaid along a transect from the dune shore to the tidal channel and measure the thickness and age (OSL, radiocarbon on shells) of aeolian sand layers; compare lagoon outlines and channel width on aerial photographs and satellite images from the 1950s onward; measure sand flux across the dune front with traps and repeat drone surveys; test whether gypsum cementation has begun in the inland sabkha of the khor, as it did 6,000 years ago at Mesaieed.","disc":["Rival explanation 1: tidal currents through the 10 km channel flush out most sand that enters, so the lagoon is in balance and not filling.","Rival explanation 2: the Mesaieed sabkha filled mainly because of falling sea level after the mid-Holocene high stand, not because of dune sand, and Khor Al Adaid will persist as long as sea level holds.","Rival explanation 3: the dune supply will run out before the lagoon fills, leaving the khor open indefinitely."],"refs":["Didi-Ooi, S.M., Whitaker, F., Jameson, J. & Strohmenger, C.J. (2014). Mesaieed sabkha, southeast Qatar: evaporite diagenesis in a prograding coastal plain. AAPG ACE, Houston.","James, B. (2011). Dunes: shifting sands of time. Gulf Times, 13 March 2011.","Al-Awah, H. & Matter, W. S. (2023). Physicochemical characterization and origin of aeolian sand dunes in southeastern Qatar. Advances in Geosciences 63, 1–13.","UNESCO World Heritage Centre. Khor Al-Adaid natural reserve, Tentative List ref. 5317 (2008).","Kirkham, A. (1998). Pleistocene carbonate seif dunes and their role in the development of complex past and present coastlines of the U.A.E. GeoArabia 3(1), 19–32."],"ic":"spark","era":"?","te":"Is Khor Al Adaid Becoming the Next Mesaieed Sabkha?","ta":"هل يتحول خور العديد إلى سبخة مسيعيد التالية؟","se":"The dunes spilling into the Inland Sea may be slowly turning it into a salt flat, exactly as a lagoon at Mesaieed was filled over 6,000 years, and the nearly exhausted sand supply sets the clock. Nobody has tested this yet.","sa":"قد تكون الكثبان المنهالة في البحر الداخلي تحوّله ببطء إلى سبخة، تمامًا كما امتلأت بحيرة شاطئية في مسيعيد على مدى 6,000 عام، ومخزون الرمل الذي يكاد ينفد هو الذي يضبط الساعة. لم يختبر أحد ذلك بعد.","na":"البحيرة التي تملؤها الكثبان","t0":6000,"t1":0,"spec":{"bas":"link","key":"dune-fill","sty":"ai-dune-fill","spot":{"en":"Born from three records in a line on this map. Mesaieed Sabkha’s record calls it a lagoon that filled with wind-blown sand over 6,000 years. The Mesaieed dunes’ record says the sand is marching south-east toward the Inland Sea and running out. Khor Al Adaid’s record says the dunes spill into the water and the sabkhas are already growing seaward. Past, present and future of one process, 25 km apart.","ar":"وُلدت الفكرة من ثلاثة سجلات على خط واحد في هذه الخريطة. يصف سجل سبخة مسيعيد بحيرة شاطئية ملأتها الرمال التي تحملها الريح على مدى 6,000 عام. ويقول سجل كثبان مسيعيد إن الرمل يزحف جنوبًا شرقًا نحو البحر الداخلي وإنه ينفد. ويقول سجل خور العديد إن الكثبان تنهال في الماء وإن السباخ تنمو نحو البحر بالفعل. ماضي عملية واحدة وحاضرها ومستقبلها، تفصلها 25 كم."},"lk":[["mesaieed-sabkha","A lagoon that filled with wind-blown sand over 6,000 years","بحيرة شاطئية ملأتها رمال الريح على مدى 6,000 عام"],["mesaieed-dunes","Sand marching south-east toward the Inland Sea, supply nearly exhausted","رمل يزحف جنوبًا شرقًا نحو البحر الداخلي، ومخزونه يكاد ينفد"],["khor-al-adaid","Dunes spilling into tidal water, sabkhas growing seaward","كثبان تنهال في ماء المد، وسباخ تنمو نحو البحر"],["qatar-dugongs","What lives on the seagrass that the sand would bury","ما يعيش على الأعشاب البحرية التي سيدفنها الرمل"]],"pred":{"en":"Cores from Khor Al Adaid will show aeolian sand layers thickening upward and landward, the lagoon outline on images since the 1950s will have retreated measurably along the dune shore, and gypsum crusts will be forming in the khor’s inland sabkha as they did at Mesaieed.","ar":"ستُظهر العيّنات اللبية من خور العديد طبقات رمل ريحي يزداد سمكها نحو الأعلى ونحو اليابسة، وسيكون محيط البحيرة في الصور منذ خمسينيات القرن العشرين قد تراجع تراجعًا قابلًا للقياس على شاطئ الكثبان، وستكون قشور الجبس آخذة في التكوّن في سبخة الخور الداخلية كما حدث في مسيعيد."},"test":{"en":"Core and date the lagoon floor, compare shoreline and channel width on seventy years of images, measure sand flux across the dune front and look for gypsum cement in the inland sabkha.","ar":"أخذ عيّنات لبية من قاع البحيرة وتأريخها، ومقارنة خط الشاطئ وعرض القناة في صور سبعين عامًا، وقياس تدفق الرمل عبر جبهة الكثبان، والبحث عن إسمنت الجبس في السبخة الداخلية."},"wrong":{"en":"If the lagoon floor holds little aeolian sand, the shoreline has not moved since the 1950s, or the tide carries out as much sand as the wind brings in, the filling idea fails.","ar":"إذا كان قاع البحيرة لا يحوي إلا القليل من الرمل الريحي، أو لم يتحرك خط الشاطئ منذ الخمسينيات، أو كان المد يُخرج من الرمل بقدر ما تُدخل الريح، تسقط فكرة الامتلاء."}}},{"id":"spec-qa-purple-basins","n":"Purple Again on the North-West Coast?","c":"speculative","s":"Cross-category hypothesis","lat":25.85,"lng":51.21,"p":"Kassite (Late Bronze Age) and Early Islamic (Abbasid)","dr":"c. 1400 BC – 885 AD (test window)","el":"","desc":"A connection between two workshops on this map, about 60 km and two thousand years apart. On Al Khor Island, about 3,000,000 crushed murex shells (Hexaplex trunculus) with Kassite pottery of about 1400–1100 BC are claimed as the earliest known production of shellfish purple. At Ain Mohammed, an Abbasid settlement of about 780–830 AD near Freiha, the main building was planned as three production units of paired rooms with gypsum-plastered basins and platforms, drains through the walls and no kitchens, and the excavators propose textile work such as washing and dyeing wool. The idea: the dye used in those basins was shellfish purple, the craft first known on Al Khor Island, revived on the north-west coast in the Abbasid period, and its chemical trace is still sealed in the plaster. Murwab, an ordinary village of the same century 16 km to the south, is the control.","sig":"If true, it would show that Qatar made one of the most valued colours of the ancient and medieval world in two separate eras, and it would turn Ain Mohammed from a probable textile workshop into a named industry with a product that could be traced in Abbasid trade.","ex":"","conf":"Untested AI speculation (connection between published records)","ev":"Al Khor Island: about 3,000,000 crushed murex shells and Kassite potsherds, c. 1400–1100 BC; Dilmun pottery c. 2000–1750 BC; Sasanian and late Islamic material shows later use (Carter & Killick 2014). Ain Mohammed: c. 780–830 AD; three production units of paired rooms with gypsum-plastered basins and platforms, drains through the walls, no kitchens; textile work such as washing and dyeing wool proposed; food remains include dates, wheat, barley, dugong and large quantities of pearl oysters; pottery mainly from southern Iraq (Qatar Museums, Landscapes of Faith 2024). Murwab: 250 houses, two mosques and a fort, 6,948 potsherds mostly 805–885 AD (Guérin & Al-Naimi 2010). Distances: Al Khor Island to Ain Mohammed about 60 km; Ain Mohammed to Murwab about 16 km. No record mentions murex at Ain Mohammed.","an":"Cooksey (2001), in a review in Molecules, showed that the main colouring compound of shellfish purple is 6,6′-dibromoindigo, a bromine-bearing molecule made by murex snails and not by the plants that give indigo or red dyes; because the bromine is a chemical fingerprint, the compound can be identified in dyed material and separated from plant dyes by laboratory analysis.","val":"Scrape plaster from the basins, platforms and wall drains of the three production units at Ain Mohammed and analyse it by chromatography and mass spectrometry for 6,6′-dibromoindigo and related brominated indigoids, and for plant-dye markers such as indigotin and alizarin; sieve the site’s middens for fragments of murex shell and compare them with the crushed Hexaplex trunculus of Al Khor Island; run the same analyses on house floors at Murwab as a control; look for murex crushing in the Sasanian and late Islamic layers of Al Khor Island to test whether the craft ever stopped.","disc":["Rival explanation 1: the basins were used for plant dyes such as indigo or madder, or only for washing and fulling wool, and no shellfish purple was made.","Rival explanation 2: the paired rooms processed food, not cloth: the record lists dates and large quantities of pearl oysters, and the basins could have served for date syrup or for opening oysters.","Rival explanation 3: purple cloth or dye was imported ready-made from Abbasid Iraq, the source of most of the pottery, so Ain Mohammed used purple without making it."],"refs":["Carter, R. & Killick, R. (2014). Al-Khor Island: investigating coastal exploitation in Bronze Age Qatar. Moonrise Press.","Qatar Museums (2024). Landscapes of Faith: Project Summary (R. Carter).","Guérin, A. & Al-Naimi, F. (2010). Preliminary pottery study: Murwab horizon in progress, ninth century AD, Qatar. Proceedings of the Seminar for Arabian Studies 40.","Cooksey, C. J. (2001). Tyrian purple: 6,6′-dibromoindigo and related compounds. Molecules 6(9), 736–769."],"ic":"spark","era":"?","te":"Were the Basins of Ain Mohammed Dyeing with Sea-Snail Purple?","ta":"هل كانت أحواض عين محمد تصبغ بأرجوان القواقع البحرية؟","se":"Three million crushed sea snails made purple on Al Khor Island in the Kassite age. Two thousand years later, plastered dye basins were built at Ain Mohammed. Perhaps they were making the same colour. Nobody has tested this yet.","sa":"صنعت ثلاثة ملايين قوقعة بحرية مسحوقة الأرجوان في جزيرة الخور في العصر الكاشي. وبعد ألفي عام بُنيت أحواض صبغ مجصّصة في عين محمد. فربما كانت تصنع اللون نفسه. لم يختبر أحد ذلك بعد.","na":"هل عاد الأرجوان إلى الساحل الشمالي الغربي؟","t0":3425,"t1":1140,"spec":{"bas":"link","key":"qapurple","sty":"ai-qapurple","spot":{"en":"Born from two records that use the same word. The Al Khor Island record describes the earliest known purple-dye works, built on a heap of murex shells. The Ain Mohammed record describes rooms with plastered basins and drains and says the team proposes washing and dyeing wool, but does not say with what. Murwab, close by and of the same century, shows what an ordinary village looked like.","ar":"وُلدت الفكرة من سجلين يستعملان الكلمة نفسها. يصف سجل جزيرة الخور أقدم مصنع معروف للصبغة الأرجوانية، قائمًا على كومة من أصداف الموريكس. ويصف سجل عين محمد غرفًا بأحواض مجصّصة ومصارف، ويقول إن الفريق يقترح غسل الصوف وصبغه، لكنه لا يقول بماذا. أما مروب، القريبة ومن القرن نفسه، فتُظهر كيف كانت تبدو قرية عادية."},"lk":[["al-khor-island","About 3,000,000 crushed murex shells, the earliest known purple","نحو 3,000,000 صدفة موريكس مسحوقة، أقدم أرجوان معروف"],["ain-mohammed","Plastered basins and drains, proposed for dyeing wool","أحواض مجصّصة ومصارف، يُقترح أنها لصبغ الصوف"],["murwab","An ordinary village of the same century, the control","قرية عادية من القرن نفسه، للمقارنة"]],"pred":{"en":"Plaster from the basins and drains of Ain Mohammed will contain 6,6′-dibromoindigo, murex shell fragments will turn up in its middens, and house floors at Murwab will show neither.","ar":"سيحوي جصّ الأحواض والمصارف في عين محمد مركّب 6,6′-ثنائي برومو النيلة، وستظهر كسر أصداف الموريكس في مكبّاته، ولن تُظهر أرضيات البيوت في مروب أيًّا منهما."},"test":{"en":"Analyse the basin plaster for brominated indigo dyes and plant-dye markers, sieve the middens for murex, and run the same tests at Murwab as a control.","ar":"تحليل جصّ الأحواض بحثًا عن أصباغ النيلة المبرومة ومؤشرات الأصباغ النباتية، ونخل المكبّات بحثًا عن الموريكس، وإجراء الاختبارات نفسها في مروب للمقارنة."},"wrong":{"en":"If the basins hold only plant dyes or no dye at all, if no murex shell is found at Ain Mohammed, or if Murwab’s floors show the same purple traces, the idea fails.","ar":"إذا لم تحوِ الأحواض إلا أصباغًا نباتية أو لم تحوِ صبغًا أصلًا، أو لم يُعثر على صدف الموريكس في عين محمد، أو أظهرت أرضيات مروب آثار الأرجوان نفسها، تسقط الفكرة."}}},{"id":"spec-qa-hyaena-coast","n":"Sea Bones in a Desert Den","c":"speculative","s":"Cross-category hypothesis","lat":25.24,"lng":50.83,"p":"Holocene to recent","dr":"Undated den bones (test window: last 10,000 years)","el":"","desc":"A connection between a ridge and a sea. Jebel Nakhsh in southern Qatar is a 33 km ridge of the Miocene Dam Formation, about 90 m high, running parallel to the west coast; weathering of its soft clays has collapsed the lower limestone into dens used by foxes and hyaenas, and Peter Andrews (2008) described cetacean bones from a possible striped hyaena den there. Off the same west coast, the shallow seagrass waters between Qatar and Bahrain hold the world’s second-largest dugong population, with 508 dugongs counted in less than 1 km² in 2015–2016. The idea: the geology made the dens, and the sea stocked them. Hyaenas scavenged marine mammals that died and washed up on the west coast and carried their bones inland, so the den is an archive of what the Arabian Gulf left on Qatar’s shore.","sig":"If true, the bone piles of a desert ridge would hold a long record of dugong and dolphin deaths on the west coast, older than any survey, and would show how closely the land and sea food webs of the peninsula were tied together.","ex":"","conf":"Untested AI speculation (connection between published records)","ev":"Jebel Nakhsh: 33 km long, about 90 m high, parallel to the west coast; Salwa, Nakhsh and Abu Samrah members of the Miocene Dam Formation; collapsed lower limestone forms dens used by foxes and hyaenas, caves first surveyed by Peter Whybrow in 1983–84 (Leblanc 2015); cetaceans from a possible striped hyaena den site (Andrews 2008). Dugongs: 508 in under 1 km², herds of 226 and 166 in December 2015, Important Marine Mammal Area of 10,676 km² between Qatar and Bahrain (Marshall et al. 2018); dugongs and dolphins of the western Arabian Gulf surveyed in the 1980s–2000s (Preen 2004). Dugong remains were also food at Ain Mohammed (780–830 AD), so people moved dugong parts too. Distance between the ridge and the centre of the dugong record: about 80 km. No record dates the den bones.","an":"Skinner, Davis and Ilani (1980) studied dens of striped hyaenas in Israel and found that the animals carry large numbers of bones back to their dens from carcasses they find across a wide area; a den’s bone pile is therefore a sample of whatever carcasses were available within the hyaenas’ range, not only of animals they killed.","val":"Re-examine the Jebel Nakhsh den bones: identify every bone to species (dugong, dolphins, whales, land animals); check whether they are fresh bone or mineralised fossil; record gnawing, digestion marks and cut marks; radiocarbon-date a sample; compare the species list and proportions with dugong and dolphin surveys and stranding records of the west coast; search other collapse dens along the ridge for the same pattern.","disc":["Rival explanation 1: the bones are Miocene fossils weathered out of the marine rocks of the Dam Formation itself and never came from the modern sea.","Rival explanation 2: people hunted or butchered dugongs and dolphins and carried parts inland, and the hyaenas only gnawed what people left.","Rival explanation 3: foxes or other scavengers, not hyaenas, built the assemblage, or the bones were washed in by rare floods."],"refs":["Leblanc, J. (2015). A Historical Account of the Stratigraphy of Qatar, Middle-East (1816 to 2015).","Andrews, P. (2008). Cetaceans from a possible striped hyaena den site in Qatar. Journal of Taphonomy 6(3–4).","Marshall, C. D. et al. (2018). Large dugong aggregations persist in coastal Qatar. Marine Mammal Science 34(4), 1154–1163.","Preen, A. (2004). Distribution, abundance and conservation status of dugongs and dolphins in the southern and western Arabian Gulf. Biological Conservation 118, 205–218.","Skinner, J. D., Davis, S. & Ilani, G. (1980). Bone collecting by striped hyaenas, Hyaena hyaena, in Israel. Palaeontologia Africana 23, 99–104."],"ic":"spark","era":"?","te":"Did the Hyaenas of Jebel Nakhsh Scavenge the West Coast?","ta":"هل نبشت ضباع جبل النخش الساحل الغربي؟","se":"Bones of sea mammals lie in a hyaena den on a desert ridge in southern Qatar. Perhaps the hyaenas fetched them from the west coast, where dugongs gather in their hundreds. Nobody has tested this yet.","sa":"ترقد عظام ثدييات بحرية في وكر ضباع على ضلع صحراوي في جنوب قطر. فربما جلبتها الضباع من الساحل الغربي، حيث يتجمع الأطوم بالمئات. لم يختبر أحد ذلك بعد.","na":"عظام بحرية في وكر صحراوي","t0":10000,"t1":0,"spec":{"bas":"link","key":"qahyaena","sty":"ai-qahyaena","spot":{"en":"Born from a paper title in one record. The Jebel Nakhsh record cites a study of cetaceans from a possible striped hyaena den, in caves made when soft clay weathered under the limestone. Whales and dolphins in a desert cave raised the question of where they came from, and the dugong record answered with the richest marine-mammal waters in the Arabian Gulf, off the same west coast.","ar":"وُلدت الفكرة من عنوان بحث في أحد السجلات. يذكر سجل جبل النخش دراسة عن حيتانيات من موقع ربما كان وكرًا للضباع المخططة، في كهوف تكوّنت حين تآكل الطين الطري تحت الحجر الجيري. أثارت حيتان ودلافين في كهف صحراوي سؤالًا عن مصدرها، فأجاب سجل الأطوم بأغنى مياه الثدييات البحرية في الخليج العربي، قبالة الساحل الغربي نفسه."},"lk":[["jebel-nakhsh","Collapse dens with cetacean bones, a possible hyaena den","أوكار انهيارية فيها عظام حيتانيات، ربما وكر للضباع"],["qatar-dugongs","The largest dugong herds off the same west coast","أكبر قطعان الأطوم قبالة الساحل الغربي نفسه"]],"pred":{"en":"The den bones will prove to be fresh, unmineralised bone of Holocene age, dominated by dugongs and dolphins of the kinds surveyed off the west coast today, and marked by hyaena gnawing rather than human cut marks.","ar":"ستتبيّن عظام الوكر عظامًا حديثة غير متحجّرة من عمر الهولوسين، يغلب عليها الأطوم والدلافين من الأنواع التي تُرصد قبالة الساحل الغربي اليوم، وتحمل آثار قضم الضباع لا آثار القطع البشري."},"test":{"en":"Identify, date and examine every bone from the dens, and compare the species list with marine-mammal surveys of the west coast.","ar":"تحديد كل عظمة من الأوكار وتأريخها وفحصها، ومقارنة قائمة الأنواع بمسوح الثدييات البحرية على الساحل الغربي."},"wrong":{"en":"If the bones are mineralised Miocene fossils, if they carry butchery marks but no gnawing, or if their species do not match the animals of the west coast, the idea fails.","ar":"إذا كانت العظام أحافير ميوسينية متحجّرة، أو حملت آثار ذبح بلا آثار قضم، أو لم تطابق أنواعها حيوانات الساحل الغربي، تسقط الفكرة."}}},{"id":"spec-qa-twin-stopover","n":"Two Man-Made Waters, One Stopover","c":"speculative","s":"Cross-category hypothesis","lat":25.1,"lng":51.3,"p":"Modern","dr":"1982 – today (test window)","el":"","desc":"A connection between the two richest bird sites on this map, both made by people and about 31 km apart on the plain south and west of Doha. Abu Nakhla is a 9 km² reservoir of treated sewage effluent created in 1982, where at least 260 bird species and about 150 greater flamingos have been recorded, and which the Public Works Authority announced in 2014 it would drain. Irkaya is a fodder farm watered by central-pivot irrigation, where 238 of Qatar’s 315 bird species had been seen by 2014, including 15 globally threatened species, with a southern farm of lagoons, reed beds and grassland. The idea: for migrating birds the two sites work as one stopover. Waterbirds move between them within a season, so the loss or shrinking of one would show up as a change in counts at the other.","sig":"If true, decisions about Abu Nakhla could not be made without counting the birds at Irkaya, and the two sites would need to be planned and protected together, as one wetland network for the migratory flyway.","ex":"","conf":"Untested AI speculation (connection between published records)","ev":"Abu Nakhla: created 1982 for treated effluent from the Doha-South and Doha-West stations; 9 km²; at least 260 bird species, about 150 greater flamingos, fish, frogs, reeds and cat’s-tail; on the Eocene Dammam Formation over a shallow aquifer; drainage announced March 2014; a Ramsar listing proposed in 2015 (Holla 2015; Elobaid et al. 2018). Irkaya: central-pivot fodder farm about 50 km west of Doha; 238 of 315 Qatari species (76%) by 2014; 15 globally threatened species including the sociable lapwing; southern farm of about 15 km² with lagoons, reed beds and grassland; listed by the Ministry as a reserve on the migratory route of birds and insects (The Peninsula 2014; QNA 2025). Distance between the two records: about 31 km.","an":"Murray and Hamilton (2010), in the Journal of Applied Ecology, reviewed the evidence that wetlands built to treat wastewater can become important habitat for waterbirds, and argued that their value for conservation depends on how they are managed and must be weighed against risks such as contaminants.","val":"Run synchronised monthly counts of waterbirds at Abu Nakhla and Irkaya through two migration seasons; ring or GPS-tag greater flamingos and selected waders at both sites and record movements between them; compare species lists and the timing of arrivals; use historical birders’ records to test whether counts at Irkaya rose in seasons when Abu Nakhla was low or disturbed; map water area at both sites from satellite images over the same years.","disc":["Rival explanation 1: the two sites offer different habitats, open water at Abu Nakhla and fields at Irkaya, so they attract different birds and do not share a population.","Rival explanation 2: the high species totals at both reflect how often birders visit them, not real use by the same birds.","Rival explanation 3: both sites are minor parts of a larger coastal network, and birds move between each site and the coast rather than between the two."],"refs":["The Peninsula (10 Mar 2014). Fauna, flora of Irkaya farm.","Qatar News Agency (15 Apr 2025). Qatar's nature reserves: promoting ecosystem sustainability and wildlife preservation (Erkia Reserve).","Holla, A. (25 Aug 2015). A case for Qatar's biggest wetland. Gulf Times.","Elobaid, E.A., Sadooni, F.N., Lebbe, L. et al. (2018). Environmental evaluation, risk assessment and management options of Abu Nakhla treated sewage effluent unlined pond. Qatar Foundation Annual Research Conference Proceedings.","Murray, C. G. & Hamilton, A. J. (2010). Perspectives on wastewater treatment wetlands and waterbird conservation. Journal of Applied Ecology 47(5), 976–985."],"ic":"spark","era":"?","te":"Do the Birds of Irkaya and Abu Nakhla Use the Two Sites as One?","ta":"هل تستعمل طيور إركية وأبو نخلة الموقعين كأنهما موقع واحد؟","se":"Qatar’s two richest bird sites, a reservoir of treated water and an irrigated farm, lie 31 km apart. Perhaps migrating birds use them as one stopover, so that what happens to one changes the other. Nobody has tested this yet.","sa":"يفصل 31 كم بين أغنى موقعين للطيور في قطر، خزان للمياه المعالجة ومزرعة مروية. فربما تستعملهما الطيور المهاجرة محطة واحدة، فيغيّر ما يحدث لأحدهما حال الآخر. لم يختبر أحد ذلك بعد.","na":"ماءان من صنع الإنسان، ومحطة واحدة","t0":44,"t1":0,"spec":{"bas":"link","key":"qawetland","sty":"ai-qawetland","spot":{"en":"Born from two records that each claim the most birds. Abu Nakhla’s record counts at least 260 species and describes a plan to drain it. Irkaya’s record calls it the richest bird site in the country, 31 km away, with lagoons and reed beds of its own. Two accidental wetlands so close together raised the question of whether the birds treat them as one.","ar":"وُلدت الفكرة من سجلين يدّعي كل منهما أكثر الطيور. يعدّ سجل أبو نخلة 260 نوعًا على الأقل ويصف خطة لتجفيفها. ويصف سجل إركية أغنى موقع للطيور في البلاد، على بعد 31 كم، وفيه بحيرات وأحراش قصب خاصة به. وأثار قرب هاتين الأرضين الرطبتين اللتين نشأتا مصادفة سؤالًا: هل تعاملهما الطيور كموقع واحد؟"},"lk":[["abu-nakhla-wetland","A 9 km² reservoir of treated water with at least 260 bird species","خزان مياه معالجة مساحته 9 كم² وفيه 260 نوعًا من الطيور على الأقل"],["irkaya-farm","238 species on irrigated fields, lagoons and reed beds","238 نوعًا في حقول مروية وبحيرات وأحراش قصب"]],"pred":{"en":"Ringed or tagged flamingos and waders will be recorded at both sites within the same season, and in seasons when Abu Nakhla’s water area shrinks, waterbird counts at Irkaya will rise.","ar":"ستُرصد طيور النحام والخواضات المحلّقة أو الموسومة في الموقعين خلال الموسم نفسه، وفي المواسم التي تنكمش فيها مساحة الماء في أبو نخلة سترتفع أعداد الطيور المائية في إركية."},"test":{"en":"Count waterbirds at both sites on the same days, tag birds at each and follow their movements, and compare counts with satellite maps of water area.","ar":"عدّ الطيور المائية في الموقعين في الأيام نفسها، ووسم طيور في كل منهما وتتبّع تنقلاتها، ومقارنة الأعداد بخرائط مساحة الماء من الأقمار الصناعية."},"wrong":{"en":"If tagged birds almost never move between the two sites, or if counts at Irkaya show no response to changes at Abu Nakhla, the two are separate stopovers and the idea fails.","ar":"إذا كادت الطيور الموسومة لا تنتقل أبدًا بين الموقعين، أو لم تتأثر الأعداد في إركية بالتغيرات في أبو نخلة، فهما محطتان منفصلتان وتسقط الفكرة."}}}],"stops":[]})}catch(e){console.error("D",String(e).slice(0,80))}
